from pack_writer import Writer
w=Writer('2026-june','-q001-060')
def add(n,topic,p,o,c,h,s,t,e,status='reviewed',track='Mathematics',difficulty='Moderate'):
 w.add(n,'Shared' if n<=20 else track,'Aptitude' if n<=20 else topic,difficulty,p,o,c,h,s,t,e,status)
add(1,'',r'There are four towns P, Q, R and T. Q is to the South-West of P, R is to the East of Q and South-East of P and T is to the North of R in line with Q, P. In which direction of P is T located?', ['South-East','North','North-East','East'],'C',['Draw Q below and left of P, and place R directly east of Q.','The line through Q and P has positive slope. Its intersection with the northward line from R lies beyond P.'],r'Take P=(0,0), Q=(-a,-b), with a,b>0. Since R is east of Q and southeast of P, R=(c,-b), c>0. The QP line is y=(b/a)x. T is directly north of R on this line, so T=(c,bc/a). Both coordinates are positive, hence T is northeast of P.',r'Compass directions can be modeled using Cartesian coordinates: east is positive x and north is positive y. A point northeast of another has both coordinate differences positive; southeast has positive x and negative y. Collinearity means the three positions lie on one straight line, expressible by a linear equation. The phrase directly north imposes equal x coordinates, whereas east imposes equal y coordinates. A useful method is to assign symbolic positive distances rather than invent equal distances that the question does not supply. Determine signs before numerical magnitudes. A common pitfall is assuming that northeast necessarily makes a forty-five-degree angle, or treating a general compass direction as an exact distance.',[(r'P=(0,0), Q=(-2,-3), and R=(4,-3). Find the point north of R on line QP.',r'Line QP is y=3x/2. At x=4 it gives y=6. Thus T=(4,6), northeast of P.'),(r'P=(0,0), Q=(3,-2), R=(-3,-2). Find the point north of R on QP.',r'The line is y=-2x/3. At x=-3, y=2, so T=(-3,2), northwest of P.')])
add(2,'',r'A certain sum of money becomes double of itself in 5 years at simple rate of interest. In how many years will it become 16 times of itself at the same rate of simple interest?',['80','70','75','90'],'C',['Simple interest grows by a fixed fraction of the principal each year.','Doubling means interest equals one principal; sixteenfold means interest equals fifteen principals.'],r'For principal P and annual rate r, A=P(1+rt). At t=5, 2P=P(1+5r), giving r=1/5. For A=16P, 16=1+t/5, hence t=75 years.',r'Simple interest is calculated on the original principal throughout the investment, not on previously earned interest. With principal P, annual fractional rate r and time t in years, interest is Prt and total amount is P(1+rt). Thus the amount is an affine, rather than exponential, function of time. If the amount reaches m times the principal in time T, then r=(m-1)/T. Another target multiple n takes T(n-1)/(m-1) years. All times must use the same units as the quoted rate. The crucial distinction is between interest and total amount: a sixteenfold amount contains only fifteen principals of interest. Applying repeated doubling instead would incorrectly assume compound interest.',[(r'A principal triples in 8 years at simple interest. When does it become sevenfold?',r'8r=2 gives r=1/4. Sevenfold requires rt=6, so t=24 years.'),(r'A principal increases to 1.5 times itself in 4 years. Find its multiple after 12 years.',r'4r=0.5, hence r=1/8. At 12 years, A/P=1+12/8=2.5.')])
add(3,'',r'In which of the following, entropy will decrease?',['Water is boiled','Sugar is dissolved in water','Ice is heated to melt','Egg is boiled in water'],[],['Identify the system whose thermodynamic entropy is being compared.','Heating, protein unfolding and aggregation compete during cooking; a visibly rigid egg does not establish an entropy decrease.'],r'The source does not specify a system, initial/final temperatures or conditions. Heating water and melting ice normally increase the material entropy; dissolution is ordinarily associated with increased mixing entropy, though a complete thermodynamic statement needs conditions. The fourth choice is often intended as a qualitative claim that coagulation makes an egg more ordered. That is not a sufficient thermodynamic derivation: boiling includes heating, unfolding proteins and aggregation, and the net egg entropy cannot be determined from the words alone. For an isolated egg-water-environment system the total entropy cannot decrease. Therefore no option is rigorously implied by the literal source, and no answer is assigned. Conditional intended reading: if “entropy” is only a simplified measure of structural ordering during coagulation and heating/unfolding are deliberately ignored, the intended choice is D; that extra convention is absent.',r'Thermodynamic entropy S is a state function defined for a reversible heat transfer by dS=δQ_rev/T. The second law requires the entropy of an isolated system to be nondecreasing; a subsystem may lose entropy by exporting heat or matter. Entropy changes must therefore specify system boundaries and states. For a phase transition at equilibrium, ΔS=L/T, with positive latent heat for melting or vaporization. Heating a material with positive heat capacity produces ΔS=∫C(T)dT/T>0. Molecular ordering alone is not a reliable substitute for thermodynamic accounting, especially when denaturation, aggregation and heating occur together. The method is to identify states, include all energy and matter exchanges, and separate subsystem entropy from total entropy.',[(r'Find the entropy change when 100 J of heat reversibly melts ice at 273 K.',r'At constant transition temperature ΔS=Q_rev/T=100/273≈0.366 J/K, positive.'),(r'A body loses 60 J reversibly at 300 K to a reservoir at the same temperature. Find both entropy changes.',r'The body changes by -60/300=-0.2 J/K. The reservoir gains +0.2 J/K, so the combined reversible entropy change is zero.')],status='source_issue')
add(4,'',r'There are two numbers such that the difference between them is 10 and their ratio is 5:3. Find the product of these two numbers.',['150','375','275','80'],'B',['Represent the numbers using a common multiplier.','Their difference is two times that multiplier.'],r'Write the numbers as 5k and 3k. Their positive difference is 2k=10, so k=5. They are 25 and 15, whose product is 375.',r'A ratio a:b states that two quantities share the form ak and bk for one common scale factor k. It determines relative size but not absolute size; an additional sum, difference or product fixes k. For a positive difference d and a>b, k=d/(a-b) and the product is ab d²/(a-b)². These formulas require the quantities to be measured in compatible units. If “difference” means absolute difference, a negative common factor may also satisfy the numerical relationship, but the product remains unchanged. The reliable method is to introduce the common multiplier, translate the remaining condition into an equation, and then recover the requested quantity. Do not replace a ratio by an additive difference.',[(r'Two positive numbers have ratio 7:4 and difference 12. Find their product.',r'Numbers 7k,4k satisfy 3k=12, so k=4. Product=28×16=448.'),(r'Two numbers have ratio 2:5 and sum 42. Find them.',r'2k+5k=42 gives k=6. The numbers are 12 and 30.')])
add(5,'',r'Suppose there are 9 identical gold-coins out of which 8 coins have same weight. One coin is counterfeit and weighs slightly less than the other coins. You have a classical balance scale for weighing. What is the minimum number of weighings required to definitively isolate and identify the counterfeit coin?',['3','4','2','8'],'C',['Each balance weighing has three possible outcomes.','Divide nine coins into three equal groups; first identify the suspect group, then its suspect coin.'],r'One weighing gives only three outcomes and cannot distinguish nine possibilities, so at least two are needed. Weigh coins 1–3 against 4–6. If equal, the lighter coin is among 7–9; otherwise it belongs to the lighter pan. In the suspect triple weigh one coin against another. A lighter pan identifies the counterfeit; equality identifies the third coin. Thus two weighings suffice and are minimal.',r'A balance scale yields three distinguishable outcomes: left lighter, right lighter, or equality. A decision procedure using k weighings can have at most 3^k terminal outcome sequences, so identifying one known-light counterfeit among N candidates requires 3^k≥N. This is an information lower bound; to prove minimality one must also give a strategy attaining it. When the counterfeit is known lighter, partitioning candidates into three equal groups and comparing two groups gives a ternary search. Equality selects the unweighed group, while imbalance selects the lighter group. If the counterfeit could instead be heavier or lighter, the state space and valid strategies change. The common pitfall is applying a two-outcome binary search model to a three-outcome balance.',[(r'Identify a known-light counterfeit among 27 coins. What is the minimum?',r'At least log_3 27=3 weighings are necessary. Compare two groups of nine, then two groups of three in the suspect nine, then two individual coins in the suspect triple. Exactly three suffice.'),(r'Identify a known-light counterfeit among 3 coins.',r'Compare coin 1 with coin 2. The lighter coin is false; equality makes coin 3 false. One weighing is necessary and sufficient.')])
add(6,'',r'The largest possible triangle is inscribed in a semi-circle of diameter 14 cm. What would be the area inside the semi-circle which is not occupied by the triangle?',['35 sq. cm','30 sq. cm','28 sq. cm','48 sq. cm'],'C',['Use the diameter as base and maximize the perpendicular height.','The radius is 7 cm, so the maximal triangle has area (14)(7)/2.'],r'The maximal triangle has the diameter as base and the top of the semicircle as third vertex, giving area 49 cm². To justify maximality, slide a lowest vertex down to the diameter without decreasing area, and extend its diameter-side base to both endpoints; the resulting area is at most 14×7/2. The semicircle area is 49π/2. The remainder is 49π/2−49≈27.969 cm², which is 28 cm² to the precision of the choices (exactly 28 with π≈22/7).',r'The area of a triangle is half the product of its base and the perpendicular height. A circle of radius r has area πr², and a semicircle has area πr²/2. In a semicircle, the largest triangle uses the full diameter as base and the maximum available height r; its area is r². One can justify this by lowering the lowest vertex to the diameter, then moving diameter-side vertices to the endpoints, operations that do not reduce the available area. The uncovered area is consequently r²(π/2−1). Keep the exact π expression until numerical evaluation. A common pitfall is using the full circle area or calling the diameter the triangle height. Integer options may encode conventional rounding.',[(r'Find the maximum triangle area in a semicircle of radius 5.',r'Base is 10 and maximal height is 5, so area=10×5/2=25 square units.'),(r'Find the uncovered area for that maximal triangle when r=2.',r'Semicircle area=2π and triangle area=4. The remainder is 2π−4≈2.283 square units.')])
add(7,'',r'During the interaction of population of two different species identify the type where only one species is benefited and is detrimental to the other species.',['Commensalism and Amensalism','Commensalism and Competition','Parasitism and Predation','Parasitism and Amensalism'],'C',['Encode the effects on each species as positive, negative or neutral.','You need a positive-negative interaction in both listed examples.'],r'Parasitism benefits the parasite and harms the host; predation benefits the predator and harms the prey. Both have the (+,−) interaction pattern. Commensalism is (+,0), amensalism is (−,0), and competition is (−,−), so the other pairs do not meet the stated condition.',r'Ecological interactions classify the effect of one species on another, often using plus for benefit, minus for harm and zero for negligible effect. Mutualism has the pattern (+,+), commensalism (+,0), amensalism (−,0), competition (−,−), and predation or parasitism (+,−). These signs describe effects relative to a suitable baseline, usually survival, growth or reproduction. Predators consume prey; parasites derive resources from a host while typically maintaining a longer association. The classification is qualitative and can depend on environmental context. For a paired-choice question, both members of the option must have the required sign pattern. A frequent error is choosing any harmful interaction without checking whether the other participant actually benefits.',[(r'A tapeworm obtains nutrients from a mammal and weakens it. Classify the interaction.',r'The worm benefits (+), and the host is harmed (−). This is parasitism, a (+,−) interaction.'),(r'A bird nests in a tree without materially affecting the tree. Classify the interaction.',r'The bird benefits (+), the tree is approximately unaffected (0), so this is commensalism rather than parasitism.')])
add(8,'',r'XUR, TQN, PMJ, ______, HEB. Which of the following options will complete the above series by replacing the question mark?',['KHE','LIF','LJH','MJG'],'B',['Convert letters to their alphabet positions.','Each letter column moves backward by four positions per term.'],r'Using A=1,…,Z=26, the columns are X,T,P,?,H = 24,20,16,?,8; U,Q,M,?,E = 21,17,13,?,5; and R,N,J,?,B = 18,14,10,?,2. Each decreases by 4. The missing positions are 12,9,6, namely L,I,F. Thus B is correct.',r'Letter-series questions can be analyzed by assigning letters their ordered alphabet positions. For grouped terms, examine each position across terms separately, then verify that the same proposed rule reproduces every given term. An arithmetic progression has a constant successive difference d, with nth value a+(n−1)d. Here each column is an arithmetic progression with d=−4, and the three letters within a term also differ by −3. The method is to infer the minimal regular rule supported by the data, compute the missing positions, convert them back to letters and check the terminal term. Finite sequences admit many artificial extensions, so ordinary aptitude conventions favor the simple repeated pattern. Avoid focusing on only one column.',[(r'Complete WTP, SO L (written SOL), OKH, ___.',r'The first letters are 23,19,15, so next is 11=K; second letters 20,15,11 do not have the same difference, so the proposed sequence is inconsistent as written and cannot support a common −4 rule.'),(r'Complete ZWT, VSP, ROL, ___.',r'Columns decrease by four: Z,V,R,N; W,S,O,K; T,P,L,H. The missing term is NKH.')])
add(9,'',r'The pie chart shows employees by department: Accounts 12%, Admin 20%, Teaching 40%, Non teaching 28%. Male:Female ratios are Accounts 7:5, Admin 2:3, Teaching 5:3, Non teaching 3:4. Total employees = 2000. What is the ratio between female employees in Admin and non teaching departments?',['3:4','5:3','2:3','4:3'],'A',['Convert each department percentage to its total employee count.','Female fractions are 3/5 in Admin and 4/7 in non teaching.'],r'Admin has 0.20×2000=400 employees, of whom 400×3/5=240 are female. Non teaching has 0.28×2000=560 employees, of whom 560×4/7=320 are female. Their ratio is 240:320=3:4.',r'A pie-chart percentage describes a fraction of the entire population, while an internal male:female ratio describes a split within one category. If the population is N, a category share is p and its male:female ratio is a:b, then the female count is Np b/(a+b). The denominator must contain both ratio parts. Ratios between category subgroups can be computed without knowing N because the common population cancels, but actual counts provide a useful consistency check. The method is to multiply the overall fraction by the conditional fraction, then simplify the resulting ratio. Avoid interpreting a female ratio part as a percentage or comparing department totals when the question asks specifically about female employees.',[(r'Of 1000 employees, 30% are in sales with male:female=2:3. How many female sales employees?',r'Sales total=300; female fraction=3/5, so females=300×3/5=180.'),(r'Two departments contain 20% and 40% of staff, with female fractions 1/2 and 3/4. Compare female counts.',r'Counts are N/10 and 3N/10 respectively. The female-count ratio is 1:3.')])
add(10,'',r'The present population of a city is 6000. If the number of males increases by 14% and the number of females increases by 22%, then the population will become 6900. What is the present population of females in the city?',['900','700','750','850'],'C',['Let the present female population be F and male population be 6000−F.','The extra growth above 14% of everyone is 8% of F.'],r'The new total is 1.14(6000−F)+1.22F=6840+0.08F. Equating to 6900 gives 0.08F=60 and F=750. The male count is 5250; checking, 5985+915=6900.',r'When two subpopulations have different growth rates, their combined growth rate is a weighted average using original population shares. If initial counts M and F have fractional increases a and b, the new total is (1+a)M+(1+b)F. With M+F=N, this becomes (1+a)N+(b−a)F, giving a single linear equation for F when the new total is known. This requires that the stated growth accounts for the complete change and that the categories partition the population. A useful check is that the observed overall growth lies between a and b. Do not average the two rates equally unless the two original populations have equal sizes; percentage changes use their respective original denominators.',[(r'A population of 1000 grows to 1120; group A grows 10% and group B 20%. Find original B.',r'New total=1100+0.10B=1120, giving B=200 and A=800.'),(r'Groups of 300 and 700 grow by 5% and 15%. Find total growth.',r'New total=315+805=1120. Growth is 120/1000=12%, equal to 0.3×5%+0.7×15%.')])
add(11,'',r'The chart gives sector shares of a GDP of Rs.650 crore: Service 38%, Industry 28%, Agriculture 10%, Others 24%. Population is 1 billion; 80% constitute the working population engaged in sectors and contributing to GDP. Which of the following has lowest contribution to GDP?',['Service','Industry','Agriculture','Non working population'],'D',['Compare all listed options, including the population category outside the sector chart.','The stem identifies the working population as the contributors.'],r'Under the stem’s stated model, nonworking people do not directly contribute through the listed productive work, so their direct contribution is zero. The three listed working sectors contribute 38%, 28%, and 10%, all positive. Therefore nonworking population has the lowest direct contribution (D). If comparison were restricted to the sectors in the chart, agriculture would instead be the smallest sector. The answer uses the explicit working-contributor convention, not a claim that nonworking persons have no wider economic effects.',r'A percentage-share chart partitions a total into category contributions. If GDP is G and a sector share is p percent, its attributed output is Gp/100. Here the four plotted percentages add to one hundred, so the chart exhausts the stated output. Population share and output share have different denominators and cannot be directly compared; worker productivity would require sector-specific worker counts. The word contribution also needs a convention: this aptitude stem treats productive working people as the direct contributors, while nonworking population is outside that direct production model. The method is to identify the comparison set before finding a minimum. A frequent pitfall is automatically selecting the smallest plotted bar when an option describes a category outside the chart.',[(r'Sectors contribute 50%,30%,20% of a total 800. Find the smallest sector contribution.',r'The smallest share is 20%, giving 0.20×800=160 units.'),(r'A model assigns output only to 600 workers among 1000 people. Compare direct production of nonworkers with a sector producing 10 units.',r'By the stated model nonworkers produce zero direct units; zero is less than ten, so nonworkers have the smaller direct contribution.')])
add(12,'',r'Candidates in a competitive examination consisted of 60% men and 40% women. 70% of men and 75% of women cleared the qualifying test and entered the final test, where 80% men and 70% women were successful. Which statement is correct?',['Overall success rate is below 50%','Success rate is higher for women','More men cleared the examination than women','Both 1 and 2'],'C',['Multiply successive-stage fractions within each gender.','Use 1000 initial candidates to compare final counts and overall rates.'],r'For 1000 initial candidates there are 600 men and 400 women. Qualifiers: 420 men and 300 women. Final successes: 420×0.8=336 men and 300×0.7=210 women. Overall success is 546/1000=54.6%, above 50%. Male success rate from the initial group is 56%, female rate is 52.5%. Thus only statement C is correct; more men also pass the qualifying stage.',r'Success in a sequence of tests requires passing every stage. Conditional probabilities therefore multiply: P(pass both|group)=P(pass first|group)P(pass final|passed first,group). Overall success is then a weighted sum across the initial group proportions, by the law of total probability. Counts and rates are distinct: a larger group can have more successful candidates even at a lower individual success rate. To analyze an aptitude question, choose a convenient hypothetical total such as 1000, compute stage counts and verify that every percentage uses the correct reference group. Do not add the two pass percentages or apply the final-stage percentage directly to all initial candidates without accounting for qualification.',[(r'40% of candidates are in A; 50% qualify and 80% of qualifiers pass. What fraction of all candidates are successful A candidates?',r'The fraction is 0.4×0.5×0.8=0.16, or 16%.'),(r'Groups are equally sized. Their two-stage rates are 0.9×0.6 and 0.8×0.7. Find overall success.',r'Group rates are 0.54 and 0.56. Weighted equally, total rate=(0.54+0.56)/2=0.55, or 55%.')])
add(13,'',r'The graph gives investment on raw materials and sales, in crores, from 2020 to 2025. Investment: 220,450,750,660,1050,840. Sales: 400,600,1000,800,1200,920. In which year was the maximum percentage increase in sales compared to the previous year?',['2021','2023','2022','2024'],'C',['Use sales values, not investment values.','Divide each sales increase by the preceding year’s sales.'],r'Year-on-year sales changes are: 2021: (600−400)/400=50%; 2022: (1000−600)/600=66⅔%; 2023: (800−1000)/1000=−20%; 2024: (1200−800)/800=50%; 2025: (920−1200)/1200=−23⅓%. The maximum increase occurs in 2022, option C.',r'A percentage change from an old value O to a new value N is 100(N−O)/O, provided O is nonzero. The denominator is the reference value, here the immediately preceding year, rather than the new value or the series maximum. This normalization allows comparisons between changes starting from different baselines. An absolute increase of four hundred need not have the same percentage increase in different years. Negative values denote decreases and cannot exceed a positive increase in the ordinary signed comparison. In a grouped bar chart, first select the correct data series and read all relevant labels before computing successive changes. Avoid confusing investment with sales, or comparing only raw differences when the stem asks for percentages.',[(r'Sales rise from 200 to 300, then to 420. Which rise is larger in percent?',r'The first rise is 100/200=50%; the second is 120/300=40%. Thus the first rise has the greater percentage.'),(r'A value falls from 500 to 400 then recovers to 500. Find both percentages.',r'The fall is −100/500=−20%; recovery is 100/400=25%. Opposite equal absolute changes have different percentage magnitudes.')])
add(14,'',r'Two natural numbers have difference 6 and the difference between their squares is 60. Find the sum of their cubes.',['620','520','720','820'],'B',['Factor the difference of squares.','The sum of the numbers is 60/6.'],r'Let x>y. Then x−y=6 and x²−y²=(x−y)(x+y)=60, so x+y=10. Solving gives x=8 and y=2. The cube sum is 8³+2³=512+8=520.',r'The factorization x²−y²=(x−y)(x+y) converts a quadratic-looking condition into a product of the sum and difference. If a nonzero difference d and square difference K are known, the sum is K/d, and the numbers are ((K/d)+d)/2 and ((K/d)−d)/2. Natural-number requirements should be checked after solving: both expressions must be nonnegative or positive according to convention, and integral. Another useful identity is x³+y³=(x+y)³−3xy(x+y), with xy=((x+y)²−(x−y)²)/4. The method avoids unnecessary expansion or trial-and-error. A common pitfall is mistaking the difference of squares for the square of the difference; those differ by a cross term.',[(r'Natural numbers differ by 4 and their squares differ by 48. Find the numbers.',r'Their sum is 48/4=12. Solving x+y=12 and x−y=4 yields x=8,y=4.'),(r'Numbers have sum 7 and product 10. Find their cube sum.',r'Using x³+y³=7³−3×10×7 gives 343−210=133; the numbers 5 and 2 confirm 125+8=133.')])
add(15,'',r'Three alarm bells ring at intervals 30 sec, 40 sec and 45 sec respectively. They started simultaneously at 4:00 am. At what time will they all ring simultaneously?',['4:06 am','4:36 am','4:40 am','5:00 am'],'A',['Seek the first positive time divisible by all three intervals.','Use prime factorizations to compute their least common multiple.'],r'The next simultaneous ringing is after lcm(30,40,45) seconds. Factor 30=2×3×5, 40=2³×5 and 45=3²×5. The lcm is 2³×3²×5=360 seconds=6 minutes. Hence the next time is 4:06 am. The question’s usual convention asks for the next occurrence; later listed times may also be common occurrences (4:36 and 5:00), so “next” is implicit.',r'Events recurring every a,b,c units after a common start coincide exactly at nonnegative common multiples of those periods. The least positive coincidence is their least common multiple. For integers, prime factorization constructs the lcm by taking the largest exponent of each prime present in any period. In contrast, the greatest common divisor takes the smallest exponents and answers a different divisibility question. Time units must be standardized before calculation; convert seconds to minutes only after finding the common period. The method assumes exact periodic intervals and a synchronized starting instant. Aptitude formulations conventionally ask for the first later occurrence even when “next” is omitted. A pitfall is adding the periods or treating a common multiple as necessarily the least one.',[(r'Bells ring every 12 and 18 minutes from noon. When next together?',r'lcm(12,18)=36 minutes, so the next common ringing is 12:36 pm.'),(r'Lights flash every 8,12,20 seconds. Find their common period.',r'8=2³,12=2²×3,20=2²×5. The lcm is 2³×3×5=120 seconds, or two minutes.')])
add(16,'',r'A process is used to separate substances in chemical mixtures. What is this process known as?',['Distillation','Filtration','Chromatography','Crystallisation'],[],['Ask whether the stem specifies volatility, particle size, differential affinity or solubility.','Each listed process separates at least some chemical mixtures.'],r'All four options are separation processes. Distillation separates components using volatility; filtration separates suspended solids using a porous barrier; chromatography separates components by differential distribution between mobile and stationary phases; crystallisation separates a solid through solubility and crystal formation. The stem supplies no mechanism, phases or mixture type that singles out one of these. Therefore a unique answer cannot be derived. Conditional intended reading: if the omitted defining feature were differential movement/affinity in mobile and stationary phases, C would follow; if it were different boiling points, A would follow. Neither condition is printed, so this item is a source issue.',r'Mixture separation exploits differences in physical or chemical properties rather than simply naming a universal operation. Distillation uses vapor–liquid equilibrium and differences in relative volatility, usually with heat and condensation. Filtration uses a permeable barrier to retain sufficiently large suspended particles while fluid passes. Chromatography uses differing interactions with a stationary phase and a moving carrier, producing differing migration or retention times. Crystallisation exploits solubility changes and crystal formation, often by cooling or evaporation. Selecting a technique requires information about the mixture, desired purity, component stability and contrasting properties. The method in a multiple-choice definition question is to match a specific mechanism, not merely a broad purpose shared by every answer. The missing mechanism here prevents unique selection.',[(r'Which method separates sand from water through filter paper?',r'Sand is an insoluble suspended solid. The paper retains particles while water passes, so filtration is the specified process.'),(r'Which method separates ink pigments as a solvent moves along paper?',r'Pigments partition differently between paper and moving solvent, producing different travel distances. This is paper chromatography.')],status='source_issue')
add(17,'',r'In a certain code 1000 is written as 1728, 125 as 343, and 343 as 729. In the same code 512 will be written as:',['1000','1331','990','1010'],'A',['All input and output numbers are perfect cubes.','Compare the cube roots of each input-output pair.'],r'The pairs are 10³→12³, 5³→7³ and 7³→9³. The rule adds two to the cube root and cubes again. Since 512=8³, its code is (8+2)³=1000.',r'A numerical code often transforms a simpler representation of a number rather than the number directly. Perfect cubes have the form n³, and recognizing their integer cube roots can reveal a consistent rule hidden by large differences. Here the transformation on cube roots is the affine map n↦n+2, so the original-domain map is x↦(∛x+2)³ on the given positive cubes. A rule should be checked against all provided examples before extrapolating. As with any finite pattern task, infinitely many contrived functions fit the samples; aptitude convention selects the simple common transformation. The method is to test familiar powers, compare underlying bases, apply the same operation and verify that the output matches one of the options.',[(r'Under n³↦(n+2)³, encode 216.',r'216=6³, so its code is 8³=512.'),(r'Under the same code, which input maps to 1000?',r'The output cube root is 10. The input cube root is 10−2=8, so the input is 8³=512.')])
add(18,'',r'Four positive integers A≤B≤C≤D have unique mode 6 and median 7. What is the minimum possible largest value D?',['6','7','8','9'],'D',['For four sorted observations, the median is (B+C)/2.','A unique mode 6 needs repetition, but three occurrences would force median 6.'],r'The value 6 must occur at least twice; if three or four entries were 6, the median would be 6. Thus exactly two entries are 6. They cannot occupy the middle/right pair, because the median 7 requires B+C=14. Therefore A=B=6 and C=8. To keep 6 as the unique mode, D cannot equal 8; since D≥8 is integral, its least value is 9. The dataset 6,6,8,9 attains it.',r'For an ordered dataset with an even number of entries, the median is the average of its two central observations. A mode is a value with maximal frequency; a unique mode has strictly greater frequency than every other value. These are separate restrictions: the median controls central positions, while mode controls multiplicities. In a four-entry integer dataset, a unique mode normally requires one value to appear at least twice. Three occurrences force both central entries to equal that value. A useful method is to enumerate possible placements of the repeated mode under the order constraints, then impose the median equation and minimize the maximum. Do not accept a tied frequency as a unique mode; 6,6,8,8 has two modes.',[(r'Four positive integers have unique mode 4 and median 5. Minimize the largest.',r'Exactly two entries are 4, so the ordered list begins 4,4. The median gives C=6. D must exceed 6 to avoid a second repeated value. Minimum D=7, attained by 4,4,6,7.'),(r'Find median and modes of 2,2,6,6.',r'The median is (2+6)/2=4. Both 2 and 6 occur twice, so there are two modes and no unique mode.')])
add(19,'',r'P+Q means P is the brother of Q; P×Q means P is the father of Q; P−Q means P is the sister of Q. Which relation shows that I is the niece of K?',['K+Y+Z−I','K+Y×I−Z','Z−I×Y+K','K×Y+I−Z'],'B',['A niece is a female child of a sibling.','Find an option making K a sibling of I’s parent and I explicitly female.'],r'B states K is brother of Y, Y is father of I, and I is sister of Z. Thus I is female and the daughter of K’s sibling Y, so I is K’s niece. A places I among K’s siblings, without specifying I’s sex. C makes I the father of Y and hence male. D makes K the father of Y and I a sister of Y, placing I as K’s child under the usual sibling convention.',r'Coded kinship relations translate symbols into directed family statements. The expression is a chain of pairwise statements, not arithmetic with ordinary precedence. “P is brother of Q” establishes that P is male and P,Q are siblings; “P is sister of Q” establishes P is female; “P is father of Q” establishes a parent-child direction and P’s sex. A niece is a female child of one’s sibling, so both generation and sex must be proved. The method is to translate every adjacent pair and draw or mentally track the family tree. Do not infer the second person’s sex from the first person’s sibling title, and do not reverse a father relation. Standard aptitude questions assume ordinary shared parentage for stated siblings.',[(r'X is sister of Y; Y is father of Z; Z is sister of W. How is Z related to X?',r'Z is female and daughter of Y, who is X’s sibling. Therefore Z is X’s niece.'),(r'A is brother of B and B is mother of C, who is male. How is C related to A?',r'C is a male child of A’s sibling B, so C is A’s nephew.')])
add(20,'',r'Select the alternative completing Fig (X). The figure is a square split into four equal quadrants. A diamond joins the midpoints of its outer sides. The circles in the upper-left, upper-right and lower-left diamond portions have black halves respectively right, bottom and top. The lower-right quadrant is blank.',['Rising diagonal from lower-left to upper-right; circle above-left of it with black left half','Rising diagonal; circle above-left with black bottom half','Falling diagonal; circle below-left with black left half','Rising diagonal; circle above-left with black right half'],'A',['Complete the diamond’s boundary first.','Follow the half-black circle through successive clockwise quarter-turns.'],r'The missing lower-right quadrant needs a rising diagonal from the bottom midpoint of the whole square to the right midpoint. That excludes C. Circles are related by rotations about the whole figure’s center: the upper-left right-black circle rotates clockwise to the upper-right bottom-black circle, then to a lower-right left-black circle, then to the lower-left top-black circle. Only A has both the required diagonal and the left-black circle.',r'Pattern completion often combines geometric continuation with a transformation rule on interior symbols. A rotation about a center sends all positions and orientations through the same angle; rotating a half-shaded circle changes which side is black even though its circular outline is unchanged. Here the square’s four quadrants form a cycle under clockwise ninety-degree rotations, and the diagonal segments must join into a closed diamond. The method is to analyze boundary geometry independently, then check symbol placement and shading orientation using the same symmetry. A candidate satisfying only one feature is insufficient. Common pitfalls include reflecting a symbol when the pattern calls for rotation, or keeping the black half fixed relative to the page instead of rotating it.',[(r'A right-black circle is rotated clockwise by 90 degrees. Which half becomes black?',r'The right direction rotates downward, so the resulting circle is black in its bottom half.'),(r'A top-black circle is rotated clockwise by 180 degrees. Which half is black?',r'A half-turn sends the top direction to the bottom, so the bottom half becomes black. Its circular boundary stays unchanged.')])
add(22,'Multivariable Calculus',r'Let f:ℝ²→ℝ be f(x,y)=x²−4xy²+y³. The directional derivatives at (1,1) and (1,0) in direction u=αi+βj are 14/5 and 6/5 respectively. Find 25(α−β)².',['1','9','16','49'],'D',['Compute the gradient at both specified points.','The derivative at (1,0) gives α directly; substitute in the other equation.'],r'∇f=(2x−4y²,−8xy+3y²). At (1,0) this is (2,0), hence 2α=6/5 and α=3/5. At (1,1) the gradient is (−2,−5), so −2(3/5)−5β=14/5, giving β=−4/5. These components have unit norm, consistent with a direction vector. Thus 25(α−β)²=25(7/5)²=49.',r'For a differentiable scalar field f on Euclidean space, the directional derivative along a unit vector u is D_u f(a)=∇f(a)·u. Differentiability is the hypothesis ensuring that all directional derivatives arise from the same linear map. The gradient consists of the partial derivatives and points in the direction of maximal instantaneous increase. Several prescribed directional derivatives give linear equations for the components of a common direction. Solve those equations and check the unit-length condition when “direction” is conventionally normalized. A nonunit parametrization scales the derivative by its speed, so normalization must not be ignored. The method here is direct differentiation followed by a two-equation linear system, without estimating differences numerically.',[(r'For f=x²+y², find D_u f(1,2) for u=(3/5,4/5).',r'∇f(1,2)=(2,4), so D_u f=6/5+16/5=22/5.'),(r'At a point ∇f=(3,4). Find the maximum unit-direction derivative and a maximizing direction.',r'Cauchy–Schwarz gives ∇f·u≤||∇f||=5. Equality is attained at u=(3/5,4/5).')])
add(23,'Linear Algebra',r'Let P₃₀(ℝ) be the vector space of real polynomials of degree at most 30. A linear transformation T:P₃₀(ℝ)→P₃₀(ℝ) satisfies T²=0. Find the maximum possible rank(T).',['8','15','24','31'],'B',['T²=0 forces image(T) to lie inside kernel(T).','Use rank-nullity in the 31-dimensional polynomial space.'],r'The basis 1,x,…,x³⁰ has 31 elements. T²=0 implies im T⊆ker T. If rank T=r, nullity is 31−r, so r≤31−r and r≤15. This is attainable: on a basis e₁,…,e₃₁ set T(e₁₆)=e₁,…,T(e₃₀)=e₁₅, and let all other basis vectors map to zero. Then T²=0 and rank T=15.',r'A square-zero linear operator satisfies T(Tv)=0 for every vector v, so every image vector lies in the kernel. Rank-nullity states that rank(T)+nullity(T)=dim V for an operator on a finite-dimensional space. The inclusion im T⊆ker T therefore yields rank(T)≤dim V/2. A maximum-rank construction pairs basis vectors into length-two chains v↦w↦0; an unpaired basis vector maps to zero. Equivalently, its nilpotent Jordan blocks have size at most two. Remember that the space of polynomials of degree at most n has dimension n+1, because the constant polynomial belongs to the basis. A bound alone does not prove a maximum: an example attaining it is required.',[(r'Find maximum rank of a square-zero operator on ℝ⁸.',r'Image lies in kernel, so r≤8−r and r≤4. Mapping e₅,…,e₈ to e₁,…,e₄ and the latter to zero attains rank 4.'),(r'Can a square-zero operator on a 5-dimensional space have rank 3?',r'Rank 3 would give kernel dimension 2, contradicting containment of a 3-dimensional image in the kernel. Maximum rank is 2.')])
add(24,'Numerical Analysis',r'Let A=[[4,2,−1],[1,5,3],[2,2,5]]. In the Gauss–Seidel scheme for Ax=b, x⁽ᵏ⁺¹⁾=Gx⁽ᵏ⁾+c. Find trace(G).',['33/50','27/50','23/50','13/50'],'D',['Separate A into diagonal-plus-lower and strictly upper parts.','Successively substitute the newly updated first and second coordinates into later updates.'],r'For the homogeneous error update, x₁′=−x₂/2+x₃/4. Next x₂′=−x₁′/5−3x₃/5=x₂/10−13x₃/20. Finally x₃′=−2x₁′/5−2x₂′/5=4x₂/25+4x₃/25. Thus G has diagonal entries 0,1/10,4/25, and trace=1/10+4/25=13/50. The affine vector involving b does not affect G.',r'Gauss–Seidel iteration solves the equations sequentially, using newly updated coordinates as soon as they are available. If A=D+L+U with diagonal D, strictly lower L and strictly upper U, the iteration matrix is G=−(D+L)⁻¹U, assuming the diagonal entries are nonzero. The constant vector is (D+L)⁻¹b. Jacobi iteration instead uses only old coordinates and has matrix −D⁻¹(L+U), so their traces generally differ. Convergence for every initial vector occurs when the spectral radius ρ(G)<1; trace by itself is not a convergence criterion. To compute G economically, update homogeneous errors coordinate by coordinate and read off old-coordinate coefficients. Be careful to distinguish new from old variables.',[(r'Find Gauss–Seidel G for A=[[2,1],[1,2]].',r'Errors update as x₁′=−x₂/2 and x₂′=−x₁′/2=x₂/4. Thus G=[[0,−1/2],[0,1/4]], with trace 1/4.'),(r'For diagonal A=diag(2,3), what is the Gauss–Seidel iteration matrix?',r'There are no off-diagonal terms, so each update directly sets x₁=b₁/2,x₂=b₂/3. Error is eliminated in one step and G=0.')])
add(25,'Linear Algebra',r'On M₃(ℝ), define T(A)=Aᵀ. Consider S1: T is diagonalizable; S2: trace(T)=6. Which is true?',['Only S1','Only S2','Both S1 and S2','Neither S1 nor S2'],'A',['Split matrices into symmetric and skew-symmetric parts.','The transpose acts as +1 and −1 on those two subspaces.'],r'Every A has the unique decomposition A=(A+Aᵀ)/2+(A−Aᵀ)/2. The symmetric subspace has dimension 3(4)/2=6 and eigenvalue +1; the skew-symmetric subspace has dimension 3(2)/2=3 and eigenvalue −1. Their direct sum is all nine-dimensional M₃(ℝ), so T is diagonalizable. Its trace as a linear operator is 6−3=3, not 6. Thus only S1 is true.',r'A linear operator is diagonalizable if its domain has a basis of eigenvectors. Over a field of characteristic different from two, every matrix splits uniquely into symmetric and skew-symmetric parts. Transposition fixes the former and negates the latter, giving eigenspaces for eigenvalues +1 and −1. For n×n matrices their dimensions are n(n+1)/2 and n(n−1)/2, summing to n². The trace of the transpose operator is therefore n, not the dimension of its fixed space. Another proof uses T²=I: the minimal polynomial divides (t−1)(t+1), which has distinct roots over ℝ. A pitfall is confusing the operator trace with the trace of a particular input matrix or with a single eigenvalue multiplicity.',[(r'Find eigenvalue multiplicities and trace of transpose on M₂(ℝ).',r'Symmetric matrices form a 3-dimensional +1 eigenspace; skew-symmetric matrices form a 1-dimensional −1 eigenspace. Trace=3−1=2.'),(r'For T(A)=−Aᵀ on M₃(ℝ), find trace and diagonalizability.',r'Symmetric matrices now have eigenvalue −1 with multiplicity 6; skew-symmetric matrices have +1 with multiplicity 3. The operator is diagonalizable and trace=−6+3=−3.')])
add(26,'Discrete Mathematics',r'Count integer solutions of x₁+x₂+x₃=17 with 0≤x₁≤7, 0≤x₂≤8, 0≤x₃≤12.',['57','59','60','148'],'A',['Start with nonnegative solutions using stars and bars.','Subtract solutions violating each upper bound and add back overlaps.'],r'Without upper bounds there are C(19,2)=171 solutions. Violations x₁≥8,x₂≥9,x₃≥13 give respectively C(11,2)=55,C(10,2)=45,C(6,2)=15 after shifting the violating coordinate. The joint first-second violation has residual sum 17−8−9=0, so contributes one solution. Other pair and triple violations are impossible. Inclusion–exclusion gives 171−55−45−15+1=57.',r'Stars and bars counts nonnegative integer solutions of x₁+⋯+x_k=N as C(N+k−1,k−1). Upper bounds require inclusion–exclusion: the event x_i≥u_i+1 can be counted by replacing x_i with y_i+u_i+1, leaving a smaller nonnegative sum. For intersections shift every offending coordinate; if the remaining total is negative there are no solutions. Inclusion–exclusion alternately subtracts single violations, adds pair overlaps, subtracts triple overlaps, and so on. The variables must be integers and the bounds inclusive. A common pitfall is shifting by u_i instead of u_i+1, thereby excluding valid boundary values. Another is forgetting overlaps after subtracting individual violation counts, which counts doubly invalid solutions incorrectly.',[(r'Count x+y=10 with 0≤x≤6 and 0≤y≤7.',r'There are 11 unrestricted solutions. x≥7 contributes 4 and y≥8 contributes 3; their intersection is impossible. Count=11−4−3=4, corresponding to x=3,4,5,6.'),(r'Count x+y+z=4 with each variable at most 2.',r'Unrestricted count=C(6,2)=15. Each violation ≥3 leaves sum 1, giving C(3,2)=3. Pair violations impossible. Count=15−3×3=6.')])
add(27,'Operations Research',r'Primal P: minimize z=3x₁+x₂−4x₃ subject to 5x₁+x₂+2x₃≥10, x₁+2x₂−x₃≤1, and x₁,x₂,x₃≥0. The dual of P:',['Is infeasible','Is unbounded','Has finite optimal solutions','Is either infeasible or unbounded'],'A',['Try the feasible ray with x₁=x₂=0 and increasing x₃.','Weak duality rules out any feasible dual point when the primal tends to −∞.'],r'For x=(0,0,t), t≥5, both constraints hold and z=−4t→−∞. Thus the primal is feasible and unbounded below. If the dual had any feasible point, weak duality would give a finite lower bound on every primal objective, a contradiction. Hence the dual is infeasible. Directly, its variables satisfy y₁≥0,y₂≤0 and 2y₁−y₂≤−4 from the third column, impossible because the left side is nonnegative.',r'Linear-programming duality relates a primal minimization problem to a dual maximization problem. For nonnegative primal variables, dual constraints are Aᵀy≤c; a primal ≥ constraint yields a nonnegative dual multiplier, whereas a ≤ constraint yields a nonpositive multiplier. Weak duality states that every feasible dual objective is a lower bound for every feasible primal objective. Consequently, a feasible primal that is unbounded below forces dual infeasibility. The converse requires care: dual infeasibility alone does not distinguish primal infeasibility from primal unboundedness without more information. A convenient method is to find a feasible improving ray, then use weak duality or check the dual sign constraints directly. Be especially careful with mixed constraint directions.',[(r'Minimize −x subject to x≥1, x≥0. Determine the dual status.',r'The primal is feasible and unbounded below. The dual is maximize y with y≥0 and y≤−1, hence infeasible.'),(r'Minimize 2x subject to x≥3, x≥0. Find primal and dual optima.',r'Primal minimum is 6 at x=3. Dual maximizes 3y subject to 0≤y≤2, attaining 6 at y=2; weak and strong duality agree.')])
add(28,'Real Analysis',r'Let f:[0,1]→ℝ satisfy f(0)=0 and f(x)=(−1)ⁿ(1+n) for 1/(n+1)<x≤1/n, n∈ℕ. S1: ∫₀¹f(x)dx converges. S2: ∫₀¹|f(x)|dx diverges. Which is true?',['Only S1','Only S2','Both S1 and S2','Neither S1 nor S2'],'C',['Multiply each step height by its interval length.','Check the remainder when the improper-integral cutoff falls inside an interval.'],r'The nth interval length is 1/n−1/(n+1)=1/[n(n+1)]. Its signed integral is (−1)ⁿ/n. Thus the integral at endpoint cutoffs is the alternating harmonic series, which converges (to −log 2). For a cutoff within the nth interval, the omitted partial interval has absolute integral at most 1/n→0, so the full improper limit exists. For |f| the interval integrals are 1/n and their sum diverges. Both statements are true, interpreting the integrals as improper at 0; f is unbounded and is not a proper Riemann-integrable or Lebesgue-integrable function.',r'An improper integral at an endpoint is defined as a one-sided limit of integrals over compact subintervals away from that endpoint. It can converge conditionally, meaning the signed integral exists while the integral of the absolute value diverges. For a step function, interval integrals are height times width, so convergence can reduce to a series. Endpoint-cutoff series convergence alone should be supplemented by a bound on the partial final interval to establish convergence for arbitrary cutoffs. The alternating-series theorem applies when positive magnitudes decrease to zero; the harmonic series diverges. Lebesgue integrability of a real function requires absolute integrability and therefore differs from conditional improper convergence. A pitfall is calling an unbounded function properly Riemann integrable on the closed interval.',[(r'On (1/(n+1),1/n], let g=(−1)ⁿn(n+1)/n². Determine integrability near 0.',r'Each signed interval integral is (−1)ⁿ/n² and each absolute integral is 1/n². Since Σ1/n² converges and the remainder is bounded by 1/n², the improper integral converges absolutely.'),(r'On the same intervals let h=n(n+1). Does its improper integral converge?',r'Each interval contributes n(n+1)×1/[n(n+1)]=1. The total is Σ1, which diverges to +∞.')])
add(29,'Partial Differential Equations',r'The PDE uₓₓ+(cos x+sin x)uₓᵧ+(y sin x+1/4)uᵧᵧ+eˣuᵧ+x²y²uₓ+3u=0 is parabolic in S={(x,y):y=φ(x)}. Find limₓ→₀ φ(x).',['1','1/2','1/4','1/8'],'B',['For Auₓₓ+2Buₓᵧ+Cuᵧᵧ, parabolic means B²−AC=0.','Factor the resulting discriminant before taking the limit.'],r'A=1, 2B=cos x+sin x and C=y sin x+1/4. The condition 4(B²−AC)=0 is (cos x+sin x)²−4y sin x−1=2sin x(cos x−2y)=0. For nonzero x sufficiently near 0, sin x≠0 and y=φ(x)=cos x/2. Therefore lim φ(x)=1/2. At x=0 the equation is parabolic for every y, which does not affect the punctured-neighborhood limit.',r'A second-order linear PDE in two variables has principal part Auₓₓ+2Buₓᵧ+Cuᵧᵧ. Its local type is determined by the discriminant B²−AC: positive is hyperbolic, zero parabolic and negative elliptic, for a nontrivial principal part. First-order terms and the zeroth-order term do not affect this classification. The coefficient of uₓᵧ is 2B, so forgetting the factor two gives an incorrect discriminant. When coefficients depend on position, type can vary across curves and regions. The method is to isolate principal coefficients, factor the zero-discriminant equation and examine the branch near the requested point. A factor that vanishes at an isolated x may enlarge the parabolic set there without changing a limiting graph.',[(r'Classify uₓₓ+2uₓᵧ+uᵧᵧ=0.',r'A=B=C=1, so B²−AC=0. The PDE is parabolic.'),(r'Find the parabolic locus of uₓₓ+2xuₓᵧ+yuᵧᵧ=0.',r'A=1,B=x,C=y. The discriminant is x²−y, hence parabolic on y=x², hyperbolic below and elliptic above.')])
add(30,'Linear Algebra',r'Let P=[[1,−1,−1],[0,1,0],[0,1,2]], Q=[[1,1,0],[0,2,0],[0,0,1]], R=[[2,0,0],[0,1,−1],[1,0,1]]. Here ∼ denotes similarity and ≁ nonsimilarity. Which statement is true?',['P∼Q and Q≁R','P≁Q and Q∼R','P≁Q and Q≁R','P∼Q and Q∼R'],'A',['All three have eigenvalues 1,1,2; compare eigenspace dimensions for 1.','Compute ranks of P−I,Q−I,R−I.'],r'All characteristic polynomials are (λ−1)²(λ−2). P−I=[[0,−1,−1],[0,0,0],[0,1,1]] and Q−I=[[0,1,0],[0,1,0],[0,0,0]] have rank 1, so their eigenvalue-1 eigenspaces have dimension 2. Each is diagonalizable and similar to diag(1,1,2), hence P∼Q. R−I=[[1,0,0],[0,0,−1],[1,0,0]] has rank 2, so its eigenvalue-1 eigenspace has dimension 1. It is not diagonalizable, and cannot be similar to Q. Thus A.',r'Similar matrices represent the same linear operator in different bases: B=S⁻¹AS with S invertible. Similarity preserves characteristic polynomial, minimal polynomial, eigenvalue multiplicities and dimensions of eigenspaces. Equal characteristic polynomials alone do not ensure similarity because Jordan block sizes can differ. A matrix is diagonalizable exactly when its eigenspaces span the full space; the geometric multiplicity of λ is nullity(A−λI). With one eigenvalue of algebraic multiplicity two and another simple eigenvalue, the repeated eigenspace dimension decides whether a size-two Jordan block is present. The method is to compute eigenvalues, then use ranks of shifted matrices to distinguish the possible Jordan forms. Avoid concluding similarity solely from matching traces or determinants.',[(r'Are diag(1,1) and [[1,1],[0,1]] similar?',r'Both have characteristic polynomial (λ−1)². The first has a 2-dimensional 1-eigenspace and the second a 1-dimensional one. They are not similar.'),(r'Are diag(2,3) and [[2,1],[0,3]] similar?',r'The second has two distinct eigenvalues and is diagonalizable, hence is similar to diag(2,3). For example eigenvectors (1,0) and (1,1) form the change-of-basis matrix.')])
add(31,'Statistical Inference',r'X₁,…,Xₙ are sampled from f(x|θ)=θx^(θ−1), 0<x<1, θ>0. Which statement is FALSE?',['ΣXᵢ/(n−ΣXᵢ) is the method-of-moments estimator of θ','−n/ΣlogₑXᵢ is the maximum-likelihood estimator of θ','n/(n−ΣlogₑXᵢ) is the maximum-likelihood estimator of θ/(1+θ)','2n/(n+ΣlogₑXᵢ) is the maximum-likelihood estimator of 2θ/(1+θ)'],'D',['Compute Eθ[X] and maximize n log θ+(θ−1)Σlog Xᵢ.','Use invariance of maximum likelihood for the two transformed parameters.'],r'Eθ[X]=θ/(θ+1). Equating this with X̄ gives θ_MM=X̄/(1−X̄)=ΣXᵢ/(n−ΣXᵢ), proving A. Let S=Σlog Xᵢ<0. The log likelihood has derivative n/θ+S and second derivative −n/θ²<0; θ̂=−n/S, proving B. Invariance gives θ̂/(1+θ̂)=n/(n−S), proving C. Similarly 2θ̂/(1+θ̂)=2n/(n−S), whereas D has n+S in the denominator. Thus D is false.',r'Method of moments estimates parameters by equating sample moments to their theoretical counterparts. For this one-parameter density on (0,1), direct integration gives Eθ[X]=θ/(θ+1), an invertible mean relation. Maximum likelihood chooses a parameter maximizing the product density, or equivalently the log likelihood. A negative second derivative and appropriate boundary behavior establish a unique global interior maximum. The invariance principle states that applying a function g to a maximum-likelihood estimate gives a maximum-likelihood estimate of g(θ), particularly straightforward for one-to-one transformations. Logarithms of observations in (0,1) are negative, so their sign is crucial. A common pitfall is simplifying a transformed estimate with the wrong denominator sign or treating a method-of-moments estimate as the likelihood estimate.',[(r'For n=2 observations e⁻¹ and e⁻³, find θ̂_ML.',r'S=−1−3=−4, so θ̂=−2/(−4)=1/2. The log-likelihood second derivative is negative, confirming the maximum.'),(r'If X̄=3/4, find θ_MM and its transformed estimate θ/(1+θ).',r'θ_MM=(3/4)/(1/4)=3. Applying the transform gives 3/4, exactly the original sample mean.')],track='Statistics')
add(32,'Linear Algebra',r'A is a 2×2 positive definite matrix with trace(A−2I)=2 and det(A²−2I)=28. Find the determinant of the adjoint (adjugate) of A.',['4','8','16','64'],'B',['First recover trace A from the shifted trace.','Write det(A²−2I) using the sum and product of A’s eigenvalues.'],r'trace A−4=2, so s=trace A=6. Write d=det A>0. Since trace A²=s²−2d, det(A²−2I)=d²−2(s²−2d)+4=d²+4d−68=28. Hence d²+4d−96=0, with roots 8 and −12. Positive definiteness selects d=8. For a 2×2 matrix det(adj A)=det A, hence the answer is 8.',r'Trace and determinant of a two-dimensional matrix equal the sum and product of its eigenvalues, counted algebraically. The identity trace(A²)=(trace A)²−2det A follows from the characteristic polynomial or the eigenvalue relation λ₁²+λ₂²=(λ₁+λ₂)²−2λ₁λ₂. For a 2×2 matrix B, det(B−tI)=det B−t trace B+t². The classical adjugate satisfies A adj A=(det A)I and det(adj A)=(det A)^(n−1); in dimension two this is simply det A. Positive definiteness gives positive eigenvalues and positive determinant, allowing elimination of an extraneous algebraic root. Be careful that trace(A−2I)=trace A−2n, not trace A−2.',[(r'A 2×2 matrix has trace 5 and determinant 6. Find det(A²−I).',r'trace A²=25−12=13 and det A²=36. Therefore det(A²−I)=36−13+1=24.'),(r'A 3×3 invertible matrix has determinant 4. Find det(adj A).',r'The adjugate determinant formula gives 4^(3−1)=16.')])
add(33,'Stochastic Processes',r'A homogeneous Markov chain on {1,2,3,4} has transition matrix [[1,0,0,0],[1/2,1/4,1/4,0],[0,0,1/2,1/2],[0,0,1/4,3/4]]. Which statement is true?',['Only state 1 is transient','Only state 2 is transient','Both states 1 and 2 are transient','All states are recurrent'],'B',['Identify closed communicating classes.','State 2 can leave for a class from which no return to 2 is possible.'],r'State 1 is absorbing, hence recurrent. States 3 and 4 communicate and form a finite closed class, so both are recurrent. From state 2 there is a 3/4 probability of leaving in one step for state 1 or 3, and no transition from those closed classes returns to 2. The probability of returning to 2 after time zero is only P₂₂=1/4<1. Therefore only state 2 is transient.',r'States of a Markov chain communicate if each is reachable from the other with positive probability. A communicating class is closed when no transition can leave it. In a finite state space every closed irreducible class consists of positive recurrent states; an absorbing singleton is a special case. A state is recurrent if the probability of ever returning after time zero is one, and transient if that probability is smaller than one. Self-loops alone do not imply recurrence: a state may loop for a while and then leave permanently. The method is to read the directed transition graph, identify closed classes and inspect whether remaining states can escape without return. Matrix rows, not columns, encode outgoing probabilities in the stated convention.',[(r'For P=[[1/2,1/2],[0,1]], classify the states.',r'State 2 is absorbing and recurrent. State 1 returns only if it self-loops initially; once it reaches 2 it never returns. Its return probability is 1/2, so it is transient.'),(r'For P=[[0,1],[1,0]], classify recurrence and period.',r'The chain alternates deterministically. Both states return with probability one and are recurrent; returns occur at even times, so each has period 2.')],track='Statistics')
add(34,'Probability Distributions',r'X has density f_X satisfying f_X(μ+x)=f_X(μ−x), μ∈ℝ. Let Y=2μ−X. S1: f_X is also the density of Y. S2: Y∼N(μ,σ²) if X∼N(μ,σ²). Which is true?',['Only S1','Only S2','Both S1 and S2','Neither S1 nor S2'],'C',['Use the density change-of-variable formula for reflection.','A normal distribution is preserved by affine transformations with reflected mean.'],r'The inverse transformation is X=2μ−Y with absolute Jacobian 1, so f_Y(y)=f_X(2μ−y). Symmetry about μ gives f_X(2μ−y)=f_X(y), proving S1. If X is normal, Y is normal with mean 2μ−μ=μ and variance (−1)²σ²=σ². Thus S2 also holds and C is correct.',r'A distribution symmetric about μ is unchanged by reflection x↦2μ−x. For an absolutely continuous random variable, this symmetry is expressed by equality of density values at equal distances on opposite sides of μ. A one-to-one differentiable change of variable uses the absolute derivative of the inverse; the absolute value prevents a negative density under a decreasing transformation. Normal distributions are stable under affine transformations: if X∼N(m,v), then a+bX∼N(a+bm,b²v) for nonzero b. The method is to derive the reflected density and then use the given symmetry. A pitfall is treating the Jacobian as −1 rather than its absolute value or reflecting around zero instead of the supplied center μ.',[(r'X is uniform on [2,6]. Find the law of Y=8−X.',r'The reflection center is 4. Y remains in [2,6] with constant density 1/4, so it has the same uniform distribution.'),(r'X∼N(3,4). Find the law of 10−X.',r'The new mean is 10−3=7 and variance is 4. Thus 10−X∼N(7,4); reflection about 5 changes the mean unless the original mean is 5.')],track='Statistics')
add(35,'Probability Distributions',r'X∼W_p(n,I_p), n>p, and A is a p×p idempotent matrix of rank r>1. Which statement is true?',['trace(AX)∼χ²_r','trace(AX)∼χ²_n','trace(AX)∼χ²_pr','trace(AX)∼χ²_nr'],[],['A chi-square quadratic-form theorem requires an orthogonal projection, hence symmetry as well as idempotence.','Test a nonsymmetric idempotent with a nonzero off-diagonal entry.'],r'Idempotence A²=A does not imply symmetry. Take p=3,r=2 and A=[[1,0,a],[0,1,0],[0,0,0]] with a≠0, which is idempotent. Writing X=ΣⱼZⱼZⱼᵀ with independent standard normal Zⱼ gives trace(AX)=Σⱼ(Zⱼ1²+Zⱼ2²+aZⱼ1Zⱼ3). Its variance is n(4+a²), whereas χ²_(2n) has variance 4n; indeed the quadratic form can be negative. Thus D is not valid under the printed hypotheses, and no listed distribution is generally true. Conditional intended derivation: if A is additionally symmetric, orthogonal diagonalization makes A=diag(I_r,0), and trace(AX) is a sum of nr independent standard-normal squares, giving D. The missing symmetry is a genuine source issue.',r'The Wishart law W_p(n,I_p) is the distribution of a sum of n independent outer products ZZᵀ with Z a p-dimensional standard normal vector. The trace identity trace(AZZᵀ)=ZᵀAZ converts matrix functionals to quadratic forms. Such a form depends only on the symmetric part S=(A+Aᵀ)/2. A symmetric idempotent is an orthogonal projection, with r eigenvalues one and the rest zero; its quadratic form is χ²_r. Summing n independent copies gives χ²_nr. A nonsymmetric idempotent is not an orthogonal projection and its symmetric part need not be idempotent or positive semidefinite. The Gaussian formula Var(ZᵀSZ)=2trace(S²) exposes this difference. Never omit the symmetry hypothesis in applying the chi-square projection theorem.',[(r'X∼W₄(5,I₄) and A=diag(1,1,0,0). Find trace(AX).',r'A is a symmetric rank-two projection. Each of five independent normal outer products contributes two independent squares, so trace(AX)∼χ²_10.'),(r'Z₁,Z₂ are independent standard normals. Is Z₁²+2Z₁Z₂ chi-square?',r'The symmetric form matrix [[1,1],[1,0]] has eigenvalues (1±√5)/2, one negative. The form can be negative, so it cannot have a chi-square law.')],status='source_issue',track='Statistics')
add(36,'Real Analysis',r'Find the Lebesgue outer measure of {x∈[−1,1]:f(x)≥0}, where f(x)=x sin(1/x) for x≠0 and f(0)=0.',['(2/π)(1−logₑ2)','(2/π)(π−logₑ2)','(1/π)(1+2logₑ2)','(2/π)(π+logₑ2)'],'B',['The function is even, so analyze positive x and double the measure.','Transform sign intervals of sin t using t=1/x and sum their lengths.'],r'f is even. On 0<x≤1, positivity first holds on [1/π,1], since 1≤1/x≤π. Subsequent positive intervals are [1/((2k+1)π),1/(2kπ)], k≥1. Their total length is 1−1/π+(1/π)Σₖ≥1(1/(2k)−1/(2k+1)). The series equals 1−log 2 by the alternating harmonic series. Thus positive-side measure=1−log 2/π. Doubling gives 2−2log 2/π=(2/π)(π−log 2). Endpoints and zero are countable and do not affect measure.',r'Lebesgue outer measure equals Lebesgue measure for measurable sets, including countable unions of closed intervals. The function x sin(1/x), extended by zero, is continuous at zero because its absolute value is at most |x|, and is even. Its nonnegative set is therefore closed relative to the interval and measurable. To compute its measure, locate sign intervals of the sine function, invert their endpoints on the positive half-line, and add interval lengths; overlaps at endpoints have zero measure. Reciprocal substitution does not preserve lengths, so interval endpoints must be transformed explicitly. The alternating harmonic identity Σₙ≥1(−1)^(n−1)/n=log 2 evaluates the resulting series. A common pitfall is assuming equal sine half-periods correspond to equal x-interval lengths.',[(r'Find measure of {x∈(0,1]:sin(1/x)≥0}.',r'It is [1/π,1] plus the reciprocal positive intervals. The sum is 1−1/π+(1−log 2)/π=1−log 2/π.'),(r'What is the measure of the zeros of x sin(1/x) on [−1,1]?',r'The zeros are 0 and ±1/(kπ), k≥1, a countable set. Each singleton has measure zero and countable subadditivity gives total measure zero.')])
add(37,'Partial Differential Equations',r'Consider z²e^(2y)cos²x(zₓ)²+z²sin²x(zᵧ)²−3ze^(2y)sin(2x)zₓ=0, 0<x<π/2, y∈ℝ. Which statement is true?',['z=1+y is a singular solution','z=3 is a singular solution','z=y−3 is a singular solution','A singular solution does not exist'],'D',['Directly substitute the proposed nonconstant solutions.','For an envelope-type singular solution use F=F_p=F_q=0, with p=zₓ,q=zᵧ.'],r'For z=1+y or y−3, p=0,q=1, and the left side is z²sin²x, which is not identically zero, so A and C fail. z=3 solves the PDE because both derivatives vanish, but is an ordinary constant solution. For nonzero z, F_q=0 forces q=0, while F_p=0 gives p=3tan x/z. Substitution gives F=−9e^(2y)sin²x≠0 on the stated domain, so no nondegenerate envelope-type singular solution exists. The constant zero solution belongs to the degenerate factor z=0 and is not a nondegenerate envelope singular integral. Under the standard singular-integral convention D is correct.',r'For a first-order nonlinear PDE F(x,y,z,p,q)=0, a complete integral contains two independent parameters and a singular integral is commonly obtained as an envelope of that family. Necessary envelope conditions involve degeneracy of the derivative-variable equations, often written F=F_p=F_q=0 in the discriminant method. A solution merely satisfying the PDE is not automatically singular; constant solutions can be regular members of solution families. Before invoking envelope conditions, substitute each offered function and distinguish solution existence from singularity. Degenerate algebraic factors, such as a zero dependent variable annihilating every coefficient, require special care and should not be confused with a nondegenerate envelope. The method here excludes proposed nonconstant functions and shows the derivative-discriminant equations are inconsistent for z≠0.',[(r'Is z=5 a solution of p²+q²−2p=0? Is it singular by F=F_p=F_q=0?',r'It has p=q=0, so F=0. Singular conditions require p=1,q=0, but then F=−1. Thus it is an ordinary constant solution, not a discriminant singular integral.'),(r'Find the singular solution of the Clairaut ODE z=xp−p².',r'The complete family is z=cx−c². Its envelope satisfies x−2c=0, so c=x/2 and z=x²/4. Direct differentiation gives p=x/2 and verifies the equation.')])
add(38,'Partial Differential Equations',r'Let z solve x(2z+y)zₓ+y(2x²−z)zᵧ+(y+4x²)z=0, with z(x,x)=1 for x∈ℝ. If z(1,√8)=α³, find α³−4α²+2α.',['√2','2√2','3√2','4√2'],'B',['Use the characteristic equations dx/[x(2z+y)]=dy/[y(2x²−z)]=dz/[−(y+4x²)z].','Look for first integrals x²−y+z and xy²z.'],r'Along a characteristic, d(x²−y+z)/ds=0 by direct cancellation. Also d log(xy²z)/ds=(2z+y)+2(2x²−z)−(y+4x²)=0 where logarithms are valid; the polynomial product identity extends to zero coordinates. On the initial curve (t,t,1), the invariants are t²−t+1 and t³. At (1,√8,α³), they are 1−√8+α³ and 8α³. Thus t³=8α³ and real cube roots give t=2α. Equating the first invariant gives 1−√8+α³=4α²−2α+1. Rearranging yields α³−4α²+2α=√8=2√2.',r'A first-order quasilinear PDE Pzₓ+Qzᵧ=R is solved locally using characteristic curves satisfying x′=P,y′=Q,z′=R. A first integral is a function constant along each such curve; differentiating a candidate with the chain rule verifies it. Two independent first integrals describe characteristic curves in three-dimensional space. Initial data on a noncharacteristic curve determine their relationship by introducing an initial parameter, evaluating both invariants there, and eliminating that parameter. Polynomial invariants remain meaningful at zeros even when logarithmic derivations temporarily require nonzero coordinates. The method here exploits cancellations in a linear combination and a product of coordinates. A common pitfall is losing the minus sign in R or treating z(x,x)=1 as data on a horizontal line.',[(r'Solve zₓ+zᵧ=0 with z(x,0)=x².',r'Characteristics satisfy x′=1,y′=1,z′=0. Thus x−y and z are constant. On y=0 the value is (x−y)², giving z=(x−y)².'),(r'Solve xzₓ+yzᵧ=z with z(1,y)=y².',r'Characteristics give x′=x,y′=y,z′=z, so y/x and z/x are invariant. At x=1, z/x=(y/x)². Therefore z=y²/x where the initial surface generates the solution.')])
add(39,'Number Theory',r'Find the sum of all incongruent solutions of 90x≡12 (mod 102) represented by positive integers at most 102.',['334','351','367','391'],'B',['Divide the congruence by gcd(90,102).','Solve modulo 17, then lift to the six representatives modulo 102.'],r'gcd(90,102)=6, and 6 divides 12. Dividing gives 15x≡2 mod 17. Since 15×8≡1 mod17, x≡16 mod17. The six representatives are 16,33,50,67,84,101. Their sum is 6(16+101)/2=351.',r'A linear congruence ax≡b modulo m is solvable exactly when d=gcd(a,m) divides b. Dividing by d yields a congruence modulo m/d whose coefficient is coprime to the modulus and therefore has a multiplicative inverse. There is one residue class modulo m/d and exactly d distinct lifts modulo m. To list representatives in a specified interval, add multiples of m/d to the reduced solution and check the endpoints. The extended Euclidean algorithm computes inverses constructively, although small moduli often permit a quick check. The final representatives form an arithmetic progression, so their sum can be calculated from count and endpoints. Do not divide the coefficient and right-hand side while leaving the modulus unchanged.',[(r'Solve 6x≡9 mod15.',r'd=3 divides 9. Reduce to 2x≡3 mod5, so x≡4 mod5. Representatives modulo15 are 4,9,14.'),(r'Does 6x≡5 mod15 have a solution?',r'gcd(6,15)=3 does not divide 5. Therefore there are no solutions.')])
add(40,'Probability',r'What is the minimum number of subsets to add to F={∅,[0,1/2),{1}} so that it becomes a field on Ω=[0,1]?',['2','3','4','5'],'D',['A field is closed under complements and finite unions.','The two given disjoint nonempty sets and their remaining complement form three atoms.'],r'Let A=[0,1/2), B={1}, and C=[1/2,1). These are three disjoint nonempty atoms partitioning Ω. Any field containing A and B must contain C=Ω\(A∪B), and all eight unions of these atoms. The original collection contains exactly three of those eight sets: ∅,A,B. Thus five new subsets are necessary and sufficient: C,A∪B,A∪C,B∪C,Ω.',r'A field or algebra of subsets of Ω contains Ω and is closed under complements relative to Ω and finite unions. These properties also imply closure under finite intersections and set differences. When finitely many generators are given, their membership patterns divide Ω into disjoint nonempty atoms. Every set in the generated field is a union of atoms, and with k atoms there are exactly 2^k distinct unions. The smallest field containing the generators includes all such unions; counting how many are already present gives the minimum additions. Here the half-open interval endpoints matter when describing the remaining atom. Do not count only complements of the original sets and forget unions of newly created sets.',[(r'How many sets are in the field generated by one proper nonempty subset A of Ω?',r'The atoms are A and Aᶜ, both nonempty. Their four unions are ∅,A,Aᶜ,Ω, so the generated field has four sets.'),(r'Ω={1,2,3,4}, generators {1,2} and {2,3}. Find the generated field size.',r'Membership patterns distinguish {1},{2},{3},{4}, four atoms. Every subset is a union of atoms, so the field has 2⁴=16 sets.')],track='Statistics')
add(41,'Partial Differential Equations',r'A particular integral of yzᵧᵧ−3zᵧ=xy³, y>0, has form z(x,y)=(1/4)xᵃyᵇlogₑ(yᶜ), a,b,c∈ℝ. Find a+b+c.',['2','4','6','8'],'C',['The homogeneous y-operator annihilates y⁴.','A resonant forcing calls for y⁴ log y; substitute to determine its coefficient.'],r'For z=(1/4)xy⁴log y, zᵧ=(x/4)y³(4log y+1) and zᵧᵧ=(x/4)y²(12log y+7). Therefore yzᵧᵧ−3zᵧ=(x/4)y³[(12log y+7)−(12log y+3)]=xy³. Since log(yᶜ)=c log y, the specified normalized form has a=1,b=4,c=1. Thus a+b+c=6.',r'A PDE containing only derivatives in y can be treated as an ordinary differential equation in y with x as a parameter. Euler-type operators map powers y^b to scalar multiples of powers; here L[y^b]=b(b−4)y^(b−1). The homogeneous exponents are zero and four. A forcing proportional to y³ resonates with the exponent-four homogeneous solution, so multiplying y⁴ by log y produces a suitable particular integral. More generally differentiating L[y^b] with respect to b explains logarithmic resonance terms. Because y>0, real powers and log(yᶜ)=c log y are well defined. The method is to substitute and match the power and coefficient. Avoid adding arbitrary homogeneous terms when the question fixes a particular-integral form.',[(r'Find a particular solution of yz″−3z′=2y³.',r'L[y⁴log y]=4y³, so z_p=(1/2)y⁴log y gives the required 2y³.'),(r'Find a particular solution of yz″−3z′=y.',r'Try z=Cy². L[Cy²]=C×2(2−4)y=−4Cy. Thus C=−1/4 and z_p=−y²/4.')])
add(42,'Partial Differential Equations',r'Let z on ℝ² satisfy zₓₓ−2zₓᵧ+zᵧᵧ+2zₓ−2zᵧ+z=e^(x+2y), with z(0,y)=eʸ and z(1,y)=0 for all y. Find z(2,0).',['e²+1','e²−1','1','2'],'B',['Factor the differential operator as (Dₓ−Dᵧ+1)².','Set z=eʸv and use coordinates s=x,t=x+y.'],r'Writing z=eʸv gives (Dₓ−Dᵧ+1)z=eʸ(Dₓ−Dᵧ)v. In s=x,t=x+y the latter derivative is ∂ₛ, so vₛₛ=eᵗ. Hence v=(1/2)eᵗs²+A(t)s+B(t). The x=0 data give B=1. At x=1, v=0 for every t, so A=−1−eᵗ/2. At (x,y)=(2,0), s=t=2 and z=v=2e²+2(−1−e²/2)+1=e²−1.',r'Constant-coefficient PDE operators can often be factored as polynomials in commuting partial derivatives. A repeated first-order factor suggests a characteristic coordinate and an exponential substitution that removes its constant term. The chain rule must be applied after specifying the new coordinates: for s=x,t=x+y, Dₓ−Dᵧ becomes Dₛ at fixed t. The transformed equation is then an ordinary differential equation in s with functions of t as integration constants. Boundary data on parallel noncharacteristic lines determine those functions pointwise. The method is to simplify the operator first, integrate the resulting one-variable equation, and impose both boundary conditions. A pitfall is treating integration constants as numerical constants when a second independent coordinate remains.',[(r'Solve (Dₓ−Dᵧ)v=0.',r'In coordinates s=x,t=x+y the equation is vₛ=0, hence v=F(x+y) for an arbitrary differentiable function F.'),(r'Solve vₛₛ=2 with v(0,t)=1,v(1,t)=0.',r'Integrating gives v=s²+A(t)s+B(t). The data yield B=1,A=−2, so v=s²−2s+1.')])
add(43,'Real Analysis',r'For −1<x<1, find the sum Σₙ₌₁^∞ n²xⁿ.',['x(1+x)/(1−x)³','x²(1+x)/(1−x)³','x(1+x)/(1−x)²','x²(1+x)/(1−x)²'],'A',['Begin with the geometric series.','Apply x d/dx twice, differentiating inside the convergence interval.'],r'For |x|<1, Σₙ₌₀∞xⁿ=1/(1−x). Applying xD yields Σₙ₌₁∞nxⁿ=x/(1−x)². Apply xD again: Σn²xⁿ=x[(1−x)⁻²+2x(1−x)⁻³]=x(1+x)/(1−x)³.',r'A power series converges absolutely inside its radius of convergence and can be differentiated term by term there, with the derivative having the same radius. The geometric series is the basic generating function 1/(1−x) for |x|<1. The Euler differential operator xD multiplies the coefficient of xⁿ by n while preserving the exponent, so repeated applications generate sums with polynomial weights n,n² and higher powers. This is more reliable than trying to sum weighted terms directly. All resulting identities hold throughout the open convergence interval; behavior at endpoints requires separate analysis. A quick check is to compare the first nonzero coefficient, here x with coefficient one. A pitfall is differentiating without multiplying back by x, which shifts the powers.',[(r'Find Σₙ₌₁∞nxⁿ for |x|<1.',r'Differentiate the geometric series to get Σnx^(n−1)=1/(1−x)², then multiply by x. The sum is x/(1−x)².'),(r'Compute Σₙ₌₁∞n²/2ⁿ.',r'Insert x=1/2 into x(1+x)/(1−x)³: (1/2)(3/2)/(1/2)³=(3/4)/(1/8)=6.')])
add(44,'Abstract Algebra',r'G is the group of invertible 2×2 matrices over integers modulo 11. What is the order of a 3-Sylow subgroup?',['3','9','27','81'],'A',['Count ordered pairs of independent columns over the field with 11 elements.','Extract the largest power of 3 dividing the group order.'],r'The first column can be any nonzero vector, giving 11²−1=120 choices. The second must lie outside its 11-element span, giving 11²−11=110 choices. Thus |GL₂(𝔽₁₁)|=120×110=13200=2⁴×3×5²×11. A Sylow-3 subgroup has order the maximal power of 3 dividing |G|, namely 3.',r'The general linear group GL_n(𝔽_q) consists of invertible n×n matrices over a finite field. Its columns must be linearly independent. Choosing them successively gives the order (qⁿ−1)(qⁿ−q)⋯(qⁿ−q^(n−1)). For n=2 this reduces to (q²−1)(q²−q). Sylow’s first theorem guarantees a subgroup whose order is the full prime-power factor p^a of the finite group order; this is a Sylow-p subgroup. Finding its order does not require constructing it or counting how many such subgroups exist. The method is to count independent column choices, factor the resulting integer and extract the relevant valuation. Do not count all q^(n²) matrices, since singular matrices must be excluded.',[(r'Find |GL₂(𝔽₃)| and its Sylow-2 subgroup order.',r'Order=(9−1)(9−3)=8×6=48=2⁴×3. Sylow-2 subgroups therefore have order 16.'),(r'Find the Sylow-3 order in GL₂(𝔽₅).',r'Order=(25−1)(25−5)=24×20=480=2⁵×3×5. The maximal power of 3 is 3.')])
add(45,'Integral Equations',r'y solves y″−5y′+4y=0 with y(0)=0,y′(0)=−1. For u(x)=y″(x), which corresponding integral equation holds?',['u=4x+5+∫₀ˣ(5+4x+4t)u(t)dt','u=4x−5+∫₀ˣ(5−4x+4t)u(t)dt','u=−4x+5+∫₀ˣ(5+4x+4t)u(t)dt','u=−4x+5+∫₀ˣ(5+4x−4t)u(t)dt'],'B',['Integrate u once and twice, retaining the initial conditions.','Substitute y′ and y into u=5y′−4y.'],r'y′(x)=−1+∫₀ˣu(t)dt and y(x)=−x+∫₀ˣ(x−t)u(t)dt. Substitution gives u=5[−1+∫u]−4[−x+∫(x−t)u]=4x−5+∫₀ˣ(5−4x+4t)u(t)dt. This is B.',r'A differential initial-value problem can be converted into a Volterra integral equation by taking its highest derivative as the unknown. Repeated integration reconstructs lower derivatives using initial values; for a second derivative u, y(x)=y(0)+xy′(0)+∫₀ˣ(x−t)u(t)dt. This identity follows from the fundamental theorem of calculus and interchanging the triangular double integral, and extends with oriented integrals to negative x. Substituting these expressions into the ODE produces a Volterra equation whose upper integration limit is x. The initial-condition polynomial becomes the forcing term and the lower-order coefficients determine the kernel. A common pitfall is dropping the initial slope or reversing the sign of the (x−t) term.',[(r'Convert y″+y=0, y(0)=1,y′(0)=0 into an equation for u=y″.',r'y=1+∫₀ˣ(x−t)u(t)dt. Since u=−y, u=−1−∫₀ˣ(x−t)u(t)dt.'),(r'Convert y′=2y with y(0)=3 into an equation for v=y′.',r'y=3+∫₀ˣv(t)dt, so v=6+2∫₀ˣv(t)dt. Integrating or differentiating recovers y=3e^(2x).')])
add(46,'Sampling and Design of Experiments',r'A balanced one-way ANOVA table has treatment sum of squares a, df=2, mean square d, F₀=1; error sum of squares=72, df=b, mean square=e; total sum of squares=84, df=c. Find the size of each treatment group.',['5','6','7','8'],'A',['Treatment df determines the number of groups.','Recover the error mean square from F, then the error df.'],r'Treatment SS=84−72=12. Its mean square is 12/2=6. Since F=MS_treatment/MS_error=1, MS_error=6, so b=72/6=12. Treatment df k−1=2 gives k=3 groups. Error df N−k=12 gives N=15. Balanced groups therefore contain N/k=5 observations each.',r'In a one-way analysis of variance with k groups and N total observations, treatment degrees of freedom are k−1, error degrees of freedom are N−k and total degrees of freedom are N−1. Treatment and error sums of squares add to total sum of squares. Each mean square is its sum of squares divided by its degrees of freedom, and the usual F statistic is treatment mean square divided by error mean square. A balanced design has equal group size m, so N=km and error degrees of freedom are k(m−1). These algebraic table relationships do not require computing a significance threshold. The inferential F distribution additionally assumes independent normal errors with common variance. A pitfall is confusing total sample size with per-group size.',[(r'Four equal groups have error df 20. Find each group size.',r'Error df=4(m−1)=20, so m−1=5 and m=6. Total sample size is 24.'),(r'Treatment SS=30, df=3, error SS=40, df=20. Find F.',r'Treatment MS=30/3=10 and error MS=40/20=2. Thus F=10/2=5.')],track='Statistics')
add(47,'Topology',r'For topological spaces X,Y with the product topology, which condition ensures the projection f:X×Y→X is a closed map?',['Connectedness of X','Connectedness of Y','Compactness of X','Compactness of Y'],'D',['A point outside the projection has its whole Y-fiber in the complement of the closed set.','Use compactness of that fiber to extract finitely many product neighborhoods.'],r'Let C⊆X×Y be closed and x₀∉π_X(C). For every y∈Y, choose a product neighborhood U_y×V_y of (x₀,y) disjoint from C. If Y is compact, finitely many V_y cover Y. The intersection U of their corresponding U_y is an open neighborhood of x₀ with U×Y disjoint from C. Thus X\π_X(C) is open and π_X(C) is closed. Connectedness does not provide the finite subcover, and compactness of X alone does not ensure the property.',r'A map is closed when it sends every closed subset of its domain to a closed subset of its codomain. Product projections are always continuous and open, but need not be closed. Compactness of the discarded factor ensures closedness by a finite-neighborhood argument often called the tube lemma. No Hausdorff assumption on X is needed for this direct proof. The fiber {x}×Y is covered by product neighborhoods avoiding a given closed set, and compactness supplies finitely many whose X-neighborhoods can be intersected. Counterexamples with noncompact discarded factors show why continuity alone is insufficient: a closed graph can have a nonclosed projection. A common pitfall is requiring compactness of the factor retained in the projection rather than the factor being removed.',[(r'Give a closed subset of ℝ² whose projection to ℝ is not closed.',r'C={(x,y):xy=1} is closed as the inverse image of {1} under a continuous map. Its x-projection is ℝ\{0}, which is not closed.'),(r'Is projection X×[0,1]→X closed for arbitrary X?',r'Yes. The interval [0,1] is compact, so the finite product-neighborhood argument proves the projection sends closed sets to closed sets.')])
add(48,'Sampling and Design of Experiments',r'The ratio estimator is Ŷ_R=(ȳ₃/x̄₃)x̄₄. Samples of size 3 without replacement are taken from the four paired population units with y-values {c,0,1,1} and x-values {0,1,0,1}. If E[Ŷ_R]=7/8, find c.',['0','3','2','1'],'D',['There are four equally likely samples, each omitting one paired unit.','The known population auxiliary mean is x̄₄=1/2.'],r'Use the standard ratio-estimation convention: y and x are measured on the same sampled population units, with lists paired in order. Omitting units 1,2,3,4 gives Ŷ_R respectively 1/2,(c+2)/2,(c+1)/4,(c+1)/2. Their mean is (5c+9)/16. Equating to 7/8 gives 5c+9=14 and c=1. The source’s subsequent definition of ȳ₄ is irrelevant to the displayed estimator, which uses x̄₄. Independent separate sampling of x and y would be a different design and does not give this result.',r'Ratio estimation uses an auxiliary variable with a known population mean to estimate the mean of a study variable: Ŷ_R=x̄_population(ȳ_sample/x̄_sample). In the standard sampling design, both variables are observed on the same selected population units, so their sample means are dependent. Under simple random sampling without replacement, every sample subset of the specified size is equally likely. With four units and sample size three, enumeration by the omitted unit gives the exact expectation. The expectation of a ratio is generally not the ratio of expectations, and the estimator is not usually unbiased. The method must respect unit pairing and avoid zero sample auxiliary means. A pitfall is averaging numerator and denominator separately, or silently replacing paired sampling by independent samples.',[(r'Two paired units have (y,x)=(2,1),(6,2). With sample size one and known population x mean 3/2, find the expected ratio estimator.',r'The estimates are (3/2)×2=3 and (3/2)×3=9/2. Their equally weighted mean is 15/4, whereas the population y mean is 4.'),(r'All population units satisfy y=3x with x>0. What is the ratio estimator?',r'Every sample has ȳ=3x̄, so Ŷ_R=3x̄_population. This equals the true y population mean exactly, regardless of sample size.')],track='Statistics')
add(49,'Complex Analysis',r'The power series Σₙ≥₀aₙzⁿ converges at z₁=(1+i)/√2 and diverges at z₂=(1−i)/√2. What is its radius R of convergence?',['R<1','R=1','R>1','R=∞'],'B',['Both points have modulus one.','Convergence at a point bounds R below; divergence at a point bounds it above.'],r'Both |z₁| and |z₂| equal 1. Convergence at z₁ implies R≥1 because convergence at a point ensures absolute convergence at every smaller modulus. Divergence at z₂ implies R≤1 because every point strictly inside the radius converges absolutely. Hence R=1. Different behavior at different boundary points is permitted.',r'A complex power series has a radius R characterized by absolute convergence for |z|<R and divergence for |z|>R. Behavior on the circle |z|=R is not determined by the radius and may differ from point to point. The Cauchy–Hadamard formula gives 1/R=limsup |aₙ|^(1/n), with standard conventions for zero and infinity. Even without coefficients, convergence at one point gives a lower bound on R and divergence at another gives an upper bound. When the two moduli coincide, these bounds pin down the radius exactly. A common pitfall is assuming convergence at one point on a circle implies convergence everywhere on that circle; this only holds strictly inside the disk.',[(r'A power series converges at z=2 and diverges at z=3i. What can be said about R?',r'Convergence gives R≥2 and divergence gives R≤3, so 2≤R≤3; the information does not determine R uniquely.'),(r'For Σₙ≥1zⁿ/n, compare z=1 and z=−1.',r'At 1 it is the divergent harmonic series; at −1 it is a convergent alternating harmonic series. Its radius is 1, illustrating differing boundary behavior.')])
add(50,'Real Analysis',r'S1: If f∈C²[a,b], f(a)=f(b)=0 and f(c)>0 for some interior c, then f″(ξ)<0 for some ξ∈(a,b). S2: For g(x)=x²sin x, if g(h)=g(0)+hg′(ξ_h), ξ_h∈(0,h), then limₕ→₀⁺ξ_h/h=1/2. Which is true?',['Only S1','Only S2','Both S1 and S2','Neither S1 nor S2'],'A',['Use the mean value theorem on either side of the positive interior value.','Near zero, g(h) behaves like h³ and g′(ξ) like 3ξ².'],r'For S1, the mean value theorem gives u∈(a,c) with f′(u)=f(c)/(c−a)>0 and v∈(c,b) with f′(v)=−f(c)/(b−c)<0. Apply it to f′ on [u,v] to obtain f″(ξ)=[f′(v)−f′(u)]/(v−u)<0. For S2, g′(ξ)=2ξ sin ξ+ξ²cos ξ=ξ²[2sin ξ/ξ+cos ξ]. The equation gives h sin h=ξ_h²[2sin ξ_h/ξ_h+cos ξ_h]. Divide by h². Since 0<ξ_h<h→0, the bracket tends to 3 and sin h/h→1. Thus (ξ_h/h)²→1/3, giving ratio 1/√3, not 1/2. Only S1 holds.',r'The mean value theorem requires continuity on a closed interval and differentiability in its interior, and equates a secant slope with a derivative at an interior point. Applying it first to f and then to f′ transfers a rise followed by a fall into a strictly negative second derivative somewhere. Smoothness C² ensures the required continuity and differentiability. For shrinking mean-value points, asymptotic expansions or standard limits can identify their relative location. If g(x) has leading term cx^m near zero, then the relation g(h)−g(0)=hg′(ξ_h) typically gives ξ_h/h→m^(−1/(m−1)) when the leading derivative is nonzero. The method must use the actual leading order; g=x²sin x starts at cubic order, not quadratic.',[(r'For g(x)=x², solve g(h)=hg′(ξ), 0<ξ<h.',r'h²=h(2ξ), so ξ=h/2 and ξ/h=1/2.'),(r'For g(x)=x⁴, find the mean-value point ratio.',r'h⁴=h(4ξ³) gives ξ³=h³/4. Thus ξ/h=4^(−1/3), different from one-half.')])
add(51,'Real Analysis',r'On [0,1], fₙ(x)=limₖ→∞(cos(n!πx))^(2k). Let f be the pointwise limit of fₙ. Which is true?',['f is Lebesgue integrable on [0,1]','fₙ converges uniformly on [0,1]','All discontinuities of f are removable','f is differentiable everywhere on [0,1]'],'A',['The inner limit is one exactly when n!x is an integer, and zero otherwise.','Rational denominators eventually divide n!, while irrational points never satisfy this condition.'],r'fₙ(x)=1 if n!x∈ℤ and 0 otherwise, because powers of a number with absolute value less than one tend to zero. For rational x=a/b, b divides n! for all n≥b, so fₙ(x)→1. For irrational x the values are always zero. Thus f=1_ℚ on [0,1]. It is measurable and zero almost everywhere, so Lebesgue integrable with integral zero. Uniform convergence fails: for each n choose a rational whose denominator does not divide n!, giving |f−fₙ|=1. Dense rational and irrational sets make f discontinuous everywhere with no limits at those points; the discontinuities are not removable and differentiability fails.',r'Pointwise convergence fixes x before letting the sequence index tend to infinity; uniform convergence instead requires the error supremum over the domain to tend to zero. The limit of even powers of cos t detects whether |cos t|=1, equivalently t is an integer multiple of π. Factorials eventually contain every fixed integer denominator, producing the rational-indicator function in the outer limit. This Dirichlet function is Lebesgue measurable because the rational set is countable, and its integral is zero because that set has measure zero. Dense rational and irrational points prevent any one-sided interior limit and hence continuity or differentiability. A removable discontinuity requires an existing finite limit, which is absent here. Do not confuse almost-everywhere vanishing with pointwise vanishing.',[(r'What is the Lebesgue integral of 1_ℚ on [0,2]?',r'ℚ∩[0,2] is countable and has measure zero. The indicator is measurable and bounded, so its integral is zero.'),(r'Does 1_{[0,1/n]} converge uniformly to its pointwise limit on [0,1]?',r'The limit is one at zero and zero elsewhere. At x=1/(2n)>0 the error is one for every n, so convergence is pointwise but not uniform.')])
add(52,'Probability Distributions',r'f_ρ is the bivariate normal density with mean zero and covariance [[1,ρ],[ρ,1]], −1<ρ<1. Let g=(f_ρ₁+f_ρ₂)/2 with ρ₁≠ρ₂, and h=f_ρ². Which is FALSE?',['Under g both marginal densities are N(0,1)','Under f_ρ, X₁|X₂=x₂ is N(ρx₂,1−ρ²)','g is bivariate normal for all ρ₁,ρ₂','h is NOT a pdf'],[],['Test the mixture using its mixed fourth moment.','Check the exact integral of the squared normal density before asserting it can never normalize.'],r'A and B are true. The mixture has covariance ρ̄=(ρ₁+ρ₂)/2. Its E[X₁²X₂²]=1+ρ₁²+ρ₂², while a bivariate normal with covariance ρ̄ would have 1+2ρ̄²; the difference is (ρ₁−ρ₂)²/2>0. Thus C is always false. However ∫f_ρ²=1/[4π√(1−ρ²)]. When ρ=±√(1−1/(16π²)), this equals one, and h is a valid density (in fact that of N₂(0,Σ/2)). Thus D also becomes false for permitted parameters. A universally unique false statement is not available under the printed parameter range. Conditional intended reading: excluding those two exceptional correlations, D is true and C is the unique false option. No unconditional key is assigned.',r'A finite mixture of densities is a density when weights are nonnegative and sum to one, but it need not belong to the original parametric family. Matching means and covariances does not establish normality; fourth moments or characteristic functions can distinguish a normal mixture. For a centered bivariate normal, E[X₁²X₂²]=Var(X₁)Var(X₂)+2Cov(X₁,X₂)². Conditional normal laws follow from the covariance matrix through linear regression and conditional variance. Multiplying a density by itself preserves nonnegativity but changes its integral, so normalization must be checked explicitly. In dimension two, the squared normal integral depends on the determinant and can accidentally equal one. The method is to verify each universal quantifier, including exceptional permitted parameter values.',[(r'For independent standard normals with joint density f, compute ∫f².',r'The covariance determinant is one, so ∫f²=1/(4π), not one. The normalized square 4πf² is N₂(0,I/2).'),(r'Mix equally bivariate standard-normal laws with correlations 1/2 and −1/2. Is the mixture independent normal?',r'Its covariance is zero, but E[X₁²X₂²]=1+1/4+1/4=3/2. Independent standard normals have that moment one, so the mixture is not jointly normal or independent.')],status='source_issue',track='Statistics')
add(53,'Topology',r'Let B={A∈M₄(ℝ):trace(2A+3I)≠−5}. Then B is:',['Compact','Connected','Closed','Open'],'D',['Trace is a continuous linear functional.','The excluded trace level is a hyperplane, whose complement is open.'],r'trace(2A+3I)=2trace A+12, so B={A:trace A≠−17/2}. The map A↦trace A is continuous on ℝ¹⁶, and ℝ\{−17/2} is open, so B is open. It is unbounded, not compact, and not closed because points on the excluded hyperplane are limits of points in B. It has two nonempty disjoint relatively open pieces trace A<−17/2 and trace A>−17/2, so it is disconnected.',r'Finite-dimensional matrix spaces carry the Euclidean topology on their entries. Linear functionals such as trace are continuous, so inverse images of open sets are open and inverse images of closed sets are closed. A single nonzero linear-functional level is an affine hyperplane. Its complement consists of two open half-spaces and is therefore disconnected, even though each half-space is convex and connected. Compact subsets of Euclidean space must be closed and bounded by the Heine–Borel theorem. The method is to simplify the trace constraint, view it as an inverse image and separately test the other properties using explicit limits and unbounded directions. Remember that trace(cI)=cn; overlooking the matrix dimension shifts the hyperplane incorrectly.',[(r'Classify {A∈M₂(ℝ):trace A>0}.',r'It is an open half-space, hence open and convex, thus connected. It is unbounded and not compact, and its boundary trace=0 shows it is not closed.'),(r'Is {A∈M₂(ℝ):trace A=0} closed?',r'Yes: it is the inverse image of the closed singleton {0} under continuous trace. It is an unbounded three-dimensional subspace, so not compact.')])
add(54,'Complex Analysis',r'D={z∈ℂ:|z|<1}. For |α|≤1 define X_α={holomorphic f:D→D:f(αz)=f(z) for z∈D}. Which statement is true?',['For all α∈D, X_α consists only of constant functions','For some α∈D, X_α contains a nonconstant function','For each α∈D, X_α contains a nonconstant function','X_α has only constant functions for some α∈D but not all'],'A',['Iterate the functional identity for a fixed z.','When |α|<1, αⁿz approaches zero.'],r'For α∈D, repeated substitution gives f(z)=f(αⁿz) for all n. Since |α|<1, αⁿz→0. Holomorphic functions are continuous, so f(z)=f(0), proving f is constant. Every constant with value in D does satisfy the identity. Therefore A is correct. Boundary cases such as α=1 allow nonconstant functions, but the options quantify over α in the open disk.',r'Holomorphic functions are continuous, so a functional identity can often be combined with convergence of iterated arguments. Multiplication by α with |α|<1 is a contraction toward zero: its nth iterate sends z to αⁿz. If a continuous function is invariant under this map, every point shares its value at the fixed point zero. Holomorphicity is stronger than necessary for this contraction argument, although it also supports a power-series proof: coefficients satisfy aₙ(αⁿ−1)=0 and hence vanish for n≥1. The strict modulus inequality is essential; rotations on the unit circle can have very different invariants. The method is to respect the open-disk quantifier rather than import a boundary counterexample.',[(r'If continuous f on ℝ satisfies f(x/2)=f(x), show f is constant.',r'Iterating gives f(x)=f(x/2ⁿ). Since x/2ⁿ→0 and f is continuous at zero, f(x)=f(0) for every x.'),(r'Give a nonconstant holomorphic f:D→D satisfying f(−z)=f(z).',r'f(z)=z² is nonconstant and maps D to D. It is even, so it satisfies f(−z)=f(z); the multiplier −1 lies on the boundary, not in D.')])
add(55,'Regression and Multivariate Analysis',r'For even n, logistic means are π_ev=e^(β₀−β₁)/(1+e^(β₀−β₁)) and π_od=e^(β₀+β₁)/(1+e^(β₀+β₁)). Sums of y for even and odd indices are n/4 and n/8. Find the model’s odds ratio based on maximum likelihood.',['1/√2','√2','√3','1/√3'],'D',['Each index group has n/2 observations.','Fit its success proportion and recover β₁ from the two logits.'],r'With Bernoulli observations, the two group MLEs are π̂_ev=(n/4)/(n/2)=1/2 and π̂_od=(n/8)/(n/2)=1/4. Thus β̂₀−β̂₁=log 1=0 and β̂₀+β̂₁=log(1/3)=−log3, giving β̂₁=−(log3)/2. The conventional per-unit covariate odds ratio e^(β̂₁) is 1/√3, option D. The predictor is encoded −1 for even and +1 for odd, so the actual odd-versus-even group odds ratio spans two covariate units and equals e^(2β̂₁)=1/3. This distinction explains why one must use the model’s per-unit coefficient convention to match the supplied options.',r'Binary logistic regression models log[p/(1−p)] as a linear function β₀+β₁x. Exponentiating β₁ gives the multiplicative change in odds for a one-unit increase in x, with other predictors held fixed. For two unconstrained groups with a saturated intercept-and-slope model, the Bernoulli likelihood is maximized at each group’s observed success fraction. When the predictor is coded −1 and +1 rather than zero and one, the group difference is two units, so the between-group odds ratio is exp(2β₁), while exp(β₁) remains the per-unit model odds ratio. The method is to compute success proportions using group sizes, take logits and solve the two linear equations. A pitfall is dividing each group’s success count by total n.',[(r'Groups coded x=0,1 have fitted probabilities 1/2,3/4. Find e^β₁.',r'The odds are 1 and 3, so β₁=log3 and the one-unit/group odds ratio is 3.'),(r'Groups coded −1,+1 have probabilities 1/4,1/2. Find coefficient and group odds ratios.',r'The odds are 1/3 and 1, so 2β₁=log3. Per-unit ratio=e^β₁=√3, and between-group ratio=e^(2β₁)=3.')],track='Statistics')
add(56,'Topology',r'Let τ₁,τ₂,τ₃ be the smallest topologies on ℝ containing respectively all (a,b) with real endpoints, all [a,b) with real endpoints, and all [a,b) with rational endpoints, a<b. Which is true?',['ℝ is not Hausdorff in τ₃','τ₂=τ₃','τ₁⊆τ₃','ℝ is connected in τ₃'],'C',['Every usual open interval can be covered by rational-endpoint half-open intervals contained inside it.','Rational cuts give clopen sets, while arbitrary real left endpoints distinguish τ₂ from τ₃.'],r'For any real open interval (a,b) and point x inside, choose rational r,s with a<r≤x<s<b. Then x∈[r,s)⊆(a,b); their union gives (a,b), so τ₁⊆τ₃. Thus τ₃ is Hausdorff, excluding A. Rational [r,∞) is τ₃-open and its complement (−∞,r) is also open, giving a separation and excluding D. If a is irrational, [a,b) is τ₂-open but not τ₃-open: any finite intersection of rational-endpoint generators containing a also contains points below a. Thus B is false.',r'A topology generated by a family as a subbasis consists of unions of finite intersections of its members. For half-open intervals, nonempty finite intersections remain half-open intervals whose endpoints come from the original endpoint set. To compare topologies, show each basic open set of one is open in the other. Refining a Hausdorff topology preserves the Hausdorff separation property, but may destroy connectedness by creating new clopen subsets. Rational-endpoint half-open intervals contain usual open intervals as unions, yet they cannot give an irrational point a neighborhood whose minimum is exactly that point. The full lower-limit topology with arbitrary real endpoints can do so. The method combines neighborhood tests, density of rational numbers and explicit clopen cuts.',[(r'Why is the lower-limit topology on ℝ Hausdorff?',r'For x<y choose t with x<t<y. The basic neighborhoods [x,t) and [y,y+1) are disjoint, separating the points.'),(r'Is [0,∞) clopen in the rational half-open generated topology?',r'It is the union of [0,n), n positive integers, hence open. Its complement (−∞,0) is usual-open and therefore open in the finer topology. Thus it is clopen.')])
add(57,'Complex Analysis',r'φ:D→D is holomorphic on the unit disk, with φ(1/2)=0 and φ(−1/2)=4/5. Find φ(1/3).',['2/7','1/5','2/5','3/5'],'B',['Apply Schwarz–Pick to the pair 1/2,−1/2.','The prescribed values attain equality, forcing a disk automorphism.'],r'The pseudohyperbolic distance between 1/2 and −1/2 is |(−1/2−1/2)/(1−(1/2)(−1/2))|=4/5. Their image distance is also 4/5 since one image is zero. Equality in Schwarz–Pick makes φ a disk automorphism. An automorphism vanishing at 1/2 is e^(iθ)(z−1/2)/(1−z/2). At z=−1/2 its fraction is −4/5, so e^(iθ)=−1. Thus φ(z)=(1/2−z)/(1−z/2), and φ(1/3)=(1/6)/(5/6)=1/5.',r'Schwarz–Pick states that a holomorphic self-map of the unit disk contracts pseudohyperbolic distance: |(f(z)−f(w))/(1−conj(f(w))f(z))|≤|(z−w)/(1−conj(w)z)|. Equality for two distinct points forces the map to be a disk automorphism. Such automorphisms have the form e^(iθ)(z−a)/(1−conj(a)z), with |a|<1. A specified zero determines a, and a second value determines the unimodular phase. The method is to compute the two distances, recognize the equality case, then evaluate the resulting explicit Möbius transformation. The unit-disk codomain is crucial for the theorem. A common pitfall is using an arbitrary interpolating polynomial when equality imposes a rigid automorphism.',[(r'f:D→D satisfies f(0)=0 and f(1/2)=i/2. Find f(1/3).',r'Equality in Schwarz’s lemma gives f(z)=iz, since the modulus is preserved at a nonzero point. Thus f(1/3)=i/3.'),(r'Find a disk automorphism vanishing at a=1/3 and taking zero to −1/3.',r'The map (z−1/3)/(1−z/3) has the required zero and value at zero. Its phase is one, so this is the automorphism.')])
add(58,'Linear Algebra',r'In P₄(ℝ), let V={p:p′(2)=0,p(1)=p(3)} and W={p:p′(0)=p(0)=0}. Find dim(V+W).',['5','6','4','3'],'A',['Each subspace is given by two independent linear conditions.','Check the four constraints jointly or compute dim(V∩W).'],r'P₄ has dimension 5. In centered powers t=x−2, the V conditions set the coefficients of t and t³ to zero, so dim V=3. W consists of p=c₂x²+c₃x³+c₄x⁴, hence dim W=3. For such p, the V conditions are 4c₂+12c₃+32c₄=0 and −8c₂−26c₃−80c₄=0. These rows are independent, so dim(V∩W)=3−2=1. Therefore dim(V+W)=3+3−1=5.',r'Linear evaluation and derivative conditions define kernels of linear maps on polynomial spaces. Their codimension equals the rank of the condition matrix, not automatically the number of printed equations, so independence must be checked. The polynomial space P_n has dimension n+1. For subspaces V,W, the dimension formula is dim(V+W)=dim V+dim W−dim(V∩W). A convenient basis can simplify constraints: centering powers at the derivative evaluation point exposes odd/even coefficients, while vanishing value and derivative at zero means divisibility by x². The method is to compute individual dimensions and then restrict one subspace’s coefficients to the other’s constraints. A pitfall is forgetting the overlap and obtaining a sum dimension larger than the ambient space.',[(r'In P₃, V={p:p(0)=0} and W={p:p(1)=0}. Find dim(V+W).',r'Each kernel has dimension 3. Their intersection imposes two independent value conditions and has dimension 2. Sum dimension=3+3−2=4, all of P₃.'),(r'Find dimension of {p∈P₄:p(0)=p′(0)=p″(0)=0}.',r'The constant, x and x² coefficients vanish, leaving arbitrary x³ and x⁴ coefficients. The dimension is 2.')])
add(59,'Statistical Inference',r'For one observation X, test H₀:f(x)=6x⁵, 0<x<1, against H₁:f(x)=10x⁹. What is the power of the most powerful test of size 0.1?',['1−(9/10)^(3/5)','(9/10)^(5/3)','1−(9/10)^(5/3)','(9/10)^(3/5)'],'C',['The alternative-to-null likelihood ratio increases with x.','Choose an upper-tail cutoff from the null CDF x⁶, then evaluate the alternative tail x¹⁰.'],r'The likelihood ratio is (10x⁹)/(6x⁵)=(5/3)x⁴, increasing in x. By Neyman–Pearson reject for x>c. Null size is 1−c⁶=0.1, giving c⁶=0.9. Under H₁ the power is 1−c¹⁰=1−(0.9)^(10/6)=1−(9/10)^(5/3). The test is most powerful for this simple alternative, and its monotone-likelihood-ratio form also fits a larger increasing-exponent family.',r'The Neyman–Pearson lemma characterizes a most powerful level-α test of a simple null against a simple alternative by rejecting where the likelihood ratio f₁/f₀ exceeds a cutoff. If the ratio is strictly increasing in the observation, the rejection region is an upper tail; if decreasing it is a lower tail. The cutoff is selected using the null distribution so its rejection probability equals the prescribed size. Power is the rejection probability under the alternative distribution, which must be evaluated separately. For densities θx^(θ−1) on (0,1), the CDF is x^θ. No randomization is needed for a continuous distribution at a single cutoff. A common pitfall is using the alternative law to set the size threshold.',[(r'Test density 2x against 4x³ on (0,1) with size 1/4. Find power.',r'Likelihood ratio=2x² is increasing, so reject x>c. Null gives 1−c²=1/4, hence c²=3/4. Power=1−c⁴=1−9/16=7/16.'),(r'Test uniform(0,1) against density 2x with size 0.2. Find the cutoff and power.',r'Reject the largest x values: c=0.8 from uniform size 0.2. Alternative power=1−0.8²=0.36.')],track='Statistics')
add(60,'Classical Mechanics',r'Match List I: A Generalized coordinates; B Rheonomic system; C Non-holonomic system; D Conservative system. List II: I Vibrators; II Forces derivable by potential energy; III Need not be associated with three mutually perpendicular directions; IV Sphere rolling on a plane.',['A-I, B-II, C-IV, D-III','A-III, B-IV, C-I, D-II','A-III, B-I, C-IV, D-II','A-II, B-IV, C-III, D-I'],'C',['Generalized coordinates are arbitrary independent parameters rather than necessarily Cartesian axes.','Rolling without slipping has nonintegrable velocity constraints; conservative forces come from a potential.'],r'A matches III: generalized coordinates need not correspond to perpendicular Cartesian directions. D matches II: conservative forces are derived from potential energy. C matches IV: a sphere rolling without slipping on a plane has nonholonomic constraints. The remaining intended rheonomic example, a vibrator with explicitly time-dependent imposed motion, matches B to I. Therefore A-III,B-I,C-IV,D-II, option C. “Vibrators” is a shorthand here for driven time-dependent constraints, since free vibration alone does not imply rheonomic constraints.',r'Generalized coordinates are independent parameters describing a mechanical configuration, chosen to suit its constraints; angles and curvilinear coordinates need not correspond to mutually perpendicular directions. Holonomic constraints can be written as equations of coordinates and time, while nonholonomic constraints generally involve velocities and cannot be integrated into such configuration equations. A rheonomic constraint depends explicitly on time; a scleronomic one does not. A conservative force admits a scalar potential, with generalized forces obtained from its negative derivatives, and its work depends only on endpoints under the usual conditions. Rolling without slipping for a sphere provides nonintegrable velocity constraints. The method in a matching question is to anchor precise definitions first and then interpret shorthand examples consistently. Do not equate any oscillating motion with an explicitly time-dependent constraint.',[(r'A pendulum has fixed length l and angle θ. Is θ a valid generalized coordinate?',r'Yes. The length constraint reduces configuration freedom to one parameter θ. The coordinate is angular and need not correspond to a Cartesian direction.'),(r'A bead is constrained to a circle of radius R(t). Is the constraint rheonomic?',r'The equation x²+y²=R(t)² explicitly depends on time when R varies. It is holonomic and rheonomic; a constant R would make it scleronomic.')])
